(function(){
window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES = window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES || {};
window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES["liberian-law"] = {"M1:Sources of Liberian Law and the Rule of Law": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 1</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SOURCES OF LIBERIAN LAW AND THE RULE OF LAW</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The Liberian Legal System</td><td>Sources of Liberian Law and the Rule of Law</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain sources of liberian law and the rule of law using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A source of law is an authority from which a legal rule derives. In Liberia, begin with the Constitution, then identify applicable legislation, lawful regulations, and relevant Supreme Court decisions. Rule of law means that public power must have a lawful basis and be subject to legal controls.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitution</td><td>Superior framework for public power and rights</td></tr><tr><td>Policy</td><td>Administrative guidance; cannot simply override superior law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>A rule is not valid merely because a powerful person announces it. Ask who made it, under what power, when it took effect, and whether a higher rule limits it. A constitution is different from a policy statement. A regulation is different from an Act passed by the Legislature. A judgment explains how a court resolved a dispute; its controlling reasoning matters more than a newspaper headline. Keep the text and the interpretation separate in your notes.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a county office announces that residents may never question its decisions. A weak answer says officials can do whatever they want. A strong answer first identifies the decision, obtains the written rule, and tests its authority against constitutional protections and applicable statutes. The absence of a legal source is a problem to investigate, not proof that every decision is automatically void. State which document is missing before concluding.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 2 and 3. Identify the supremacy clause and the three branches of government.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both constitution and policy. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A source of law is an authority from which a legal rule derives. In Liberia, begin with the Constitution, then identify applicable legislation, lawful regulations, and relevant Supreme Court decisions. Rule of law means that public power must have a lawful basis and be subject to legal controls.</li><li>Separate constitution from policy.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitution</td><td>Superior framework for public power and rights</td></tr><tr><td>Policy</td><td>Administrative guidance; cannot simply override superior law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Constitution</summary><p>Superior framework for public power and rights</p></details><details><summary>Policy</summary><p>Administrative guidance; cannot simply override superior law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which source is the starting point when a Liberian statute conflicts with the Constitution?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Constitution</li><li>A social media poll</li><li>A private policy</li><li>The oldest newspaper</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 2 establishes constitutional supremacy.</p></details><p><strong>A ministry issues a regulation. What should a researcher check first?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its enabling legal authority</li><li>Its logo colour</li><li>Its popularity</li><li>The number of copies</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Delegated rules need a lawful source of power.</p></details><p><strong>Why is a news report insufficient as the rule for an exam answer?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It may summarise rather than establish the law</li><li>It is always false</li><li>It replaces a statute</li><li>It is a court order</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Trace the report to the primary legal text.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Constitution and Policy.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a source hierarchy chart and a 400-word analysis of the fictional county directive. Cite Articles 2 and 3 and list the facts still needed.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M1:Common Law, Statutory Law and Customary Justice": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 1</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: COMMON LAW, STATUTORY LAW AND CUSTOMARY JUSTICE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The Liberian Legal System</td><td>Common Law, Statutory Law and Customary Justice</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain common law, statutory law and customary justice using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Common law reasoning uses judicial decisions and legal principles developed through cases. Statutory law comes from legislation. Customary law concerns recognised community practices. These sources can interact, but a custom is not automatically enforceable simply because it is longstanding.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil law system</td><td>A legal tradition with extensive codification</td></tr><tr><td>Civil claim</td><td>A non-criminal dispute seeking a remedy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Liberian study requires attention to formal institutions and customary settings. Separate the question of what a community does from whether the practice is legally recognised. Identify the people, subject matter and competent institution. A foreign common-law case can illustrate reasoning but is not automatically a binding Liberian precedent. A civil-law legal system is also different from a civil lawsuit: one describes a tradition of law, the other a type of dispute.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a family says a land issue must be decided only by a village elder; another party has a court claim. Map each forum and identify the legal basis of its authority. Then check the land category, the parties, statutory safeguards and any constitutional conflict. Do not assume that customary proceedings remove access to formal justice or that all customary practices are invalid.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Identify courts and jurisdiction in the Judiciary Law. Compare these with Article 2 of the Constitution.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/judiciary-law/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Judicial laws — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both civil law system and civil claim. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Common law reasoning uses judicial decisions and legal principles developed through cases. Statutory law comes from legislation. Customary law concerns recognised community practices. These sources can interact, but a custom is not automatically enforceable simply because it is longstanding.</li><li>Separate civil law system from civil claim.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil law system</td><td>A legal tradition with extensive codification</td></tr><tr><td>Civil claim</td><td>A non-criminal dispute seeking a remedy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Civil law system</summary><p>A legal tradition with extensive codification</p></details><details><summary>Civil claim</summary><p>A non-criminal dispute seeking a remedy</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What makes a foreign judgment useful but not automatically controlling in Liberia?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its jurisdiction and authority must be assessed</li><li>Its English language makes it binding</li><li>Its age makes it legislation</li><li>Every internet case binds every court</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Jurisdiction determines the status of authority.</p></details><p><strong>Which statement correctly distinguishes civil law from a civil claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>One can describe a legal system; the other a dispute</li><li>Both always mean criminal prosecution</li><li>Both mean customary ceremony</li><li>Neither concerns law</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A legal tradition and a type of proceeding are different concepts.</p></details><p><strong>How should a longstanding customary practice be analysed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Check recognition, scope and higher-law limits</li><li>Assume it overrides all statutes</li><li>Treat it as automatically unlawful</li><li>Ignore affected persons</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> History alone does not settle enforceability.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Civil law system and Civil claim.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a two-column comparison of statutory and customary processes for a fictional land dispute, identifying the authority you would need before choosing a forum.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M2:Finding, Checking and Citing Legal Authorities": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 2</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: FINDING, CHECKING AND CITING LEGAL AUTHORITIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Research and Writing</td><td>Finding, Checking and Citing Legal Authorities</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain finding, checking and citing legal authorities using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Primary authority contains the law itself, such as a statute or judgment. Secondary material explains or comments on that law. Legal research is an iterative process: define the issue, find sources, check their status, and record how they answer the question.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary authority</td><td>Law-producing or law-declaring text</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary source</td><td>Explanation used to locate and understand primary law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Start a research log with jurisdiction, issue, search terms, source, pinpoint reference and date checked. Use a textbook to develop vocabulary, then retrieve the cited primary material. For cases, check later treatment; for statutes, check commencement, amendments and repeal. An AI answer is a research lead, never evidence that a quoted case exists. Open each authority before citing it. Save enough citation information for another learner to find the same page or provision.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an online article says a notice must be given within seven days but does not name the law. Search by the legal issue and relevant statute rather than copying the number. Record whether the article concerns Liberia and whether the text is current. If the law cannot be verified, write that the deadline is unconfirmed. Inventing a section number makes an answer less reliable than honestly identifying a research gap.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the Library of Congress guide to distinguish primary and secondary sources; locate a Liberian primary authority through the Judiciary.</p><p><a href=\"https://guides.loc.gov/law-secondary-resources\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal research: secondary resources — Library of Congress</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both primary authority and secondary source. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Primary authority contains the law itself, such as a statute or judgment. Secondary material explains or comments on that law. Legal research is an iterative process: define the issue, find sources, check their status, and record how they answer the question.</li><li>Separate primary authority from secondary source.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Primary authority</td><td>Law-producing or law-declaring text</td></tr><tr><td>Secondary source</td><td>Explanation used to locate and understand primary law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Primary authority</summary><p>Law-producing or law-declaring text</p></details><details><summary>Secondary source</summary><p>Explanation used to locate and understand primary law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which item is primary authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The text of a court judgment</li><li>A student blog</li><li>A revision mnemonic</li><li>A news comment</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The court text provides the decision itself.</p></details><p><strong>An AI supplies a case citation you cannot find. What should you do?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Verify or omit it and disclose the gap</li><li>Invent the missing paragraph</li><li>Treat fluency as proof</li><li>Attribute it to any court</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A plausible citation can still be fabricated.</p></details><p><strong>Why record a pinpoint citation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It identifies the exact relevant provision or passage</li><li>It replaces legal analysis</li><li>It guarantees victory</li><li>It makes all sources binding</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A reader must be able to check the precise support.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Primary authority and Secondary source.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a research log for a fictional unpaid-invoice claim with five entries. Include one statute, one judgment, and a clear note on any unverified amendment.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M2:Case Briefing and IRAC Legal Analysis": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 2</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CASE BRIEFING AND IRAC LEGAL ANALYSIS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Research and Writing</td><td>Case Briefing and IRAC Legal Analysis</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain case briefing and irac legal analysis using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A case brief records material facts, procedural history, issue, holding and reasoning. IRAC means Issue, Rule, Application and Conclusion. A brief explains a decision; IRAC helps organise your own analysis of a problem.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Holding</td><td>The court decision on the issue before it</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>Explanation connecting the rule to the material facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Select facts that affect the legal test. State the issue as a question, not the whole story. Give a rule with authority, then connect each legal element to evidence and competing arguments. Application is usually the longest part. Identify uncertainty rather than hiding it. Distinguish the holding from incidental comments. In a problem question, a careful conditional conclusion is better than claiming certainty when key facts are absent.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a seller promises delivery Friday; the buyer pays; delivery never occurs. Issue: did the failure breach an enforceable obligation? Rule: establish the applicable contract terms and rule for performance. Application: payment and messages may show agreement, but an extension or excuse must be investigated. Conclusion: a breach claim may exist if formation, the due date and unexcused nonperformance are established. This is reasoning; merely writing “buyer wins” is not.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the IRAC guide, then practise on a published Liberian judgment located through the Judiciary.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.monash.edu/student-academic-success/excel-at-writing/annotated-assessment-samples/law/law-legal-problem-solving-irac\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Legal problem solving with IRAC — Monash University</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both holding and application. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A case brief records material facts, procedural history, issue, holding and reasoning. IRAC means Issue, Rule, Application and Conclusion. A brief explains a decision; IRAC helps organise your own analysis of a problem.</li><li>Separate holding from application.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Holding</td><td>The court decision on the issue before it</td></tr><tr><td>Application</td><td>Explanation connecting the rule to the material facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Holding</summary><p>The court decision on the issue before it</p></details><details><summary>Application</summary><p>Explanation connecting the rule to the material facts</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which part of IRAC connects evidence to each legal element?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Application</li><li>Title page</li><li>Citation list alone</li><li>Conclusion alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Application explains why the facts satisfy or fail the rule.</p></details><p><strong>What belongs in the material-facts section of a brief?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Facts relevant to the legal issue</li><li>Every detail regardless of relevance</li><li>Only the winning party opinions</li><li>Only the judge biography</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Materiality depends on the rule being applied.</p></details><p><strong>How should an uncertain outcome be stated?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Conditionally, explaining the missing facts</li><li>As guaranteed success</li><li>Without any reasoning</li><li>By inventing evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A conclusion should reflect the available record.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Holding and Application.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a one-page case brief and a 600-word IRAC response to the delivery scenario. Include the strongest counterargument and two missing facts.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M3:Constitutional Supremacy and Separation of Powers": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 3</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONSTITUTIONAL SUPREMACY AND SEPARATION OF POWERS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitutional Law</td><td>Constitutional Supremacy and Separation of Powers</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain constitutional supremacy and separation of powers using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Separation of powers allocates governmental functions among institutions. Constitutional supremacy requires those institutions to operate within the Constitution. Judicial review examines consistency with constitutional requirements; it is different from voting on whether a policy is popular.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Judicial review</td><td>Legal assessment of an act against superior legal requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal</td><td>Review through the procedure and court authorised by law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>When analysing an institutional dispute, name the actor and function first. Then locate the provision granting power and any provision limiting it. Consider whether the alleged act is legislative, executive or judicial. Do not import a foreign constitution clause into Liberia. A court structure diagram should show who decides cases and how review works, without inventing appellate routes or jurisdictional thresholds.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a government department announces that it may reverse any court judgment by letter. The problem is institutional authority, not whether the department agrees with the outcome. Compare its claimed power with Articles 65 and 66 and the relevant procedural rules. Ask whether a lawful appeal or other remedy exists. A strong answer identifies the constitutional tension and the proper source for review rather than encouraging disobedience to an order.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 2, 3 and 65–66; identify judicial power and final appellate jurisdiction.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both judicial review and appeal. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Separation of powers allocates governmental functions among institutions. Constitutional supremacy requires those institutions to operate within the Constitution. Judicial review examines consistency with constitutional requirements; it is different from voting on whether a policy is popular.</li><li>Separate judicial review from appeal.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Judicial review</td><td>Legal assessment of an act against superior legal requirements</td></tr><tr><td>Appeal</td><td>Review through the procedure and court authorised by law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Judicial review</summary><p>Legal assessment of an act against superior legal requirements</p></details><details><summary>Appeal</summary><p>Review through the procedure and court authorised by law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which source should define the powers of Liberia government branches?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Liberian Constitution</li><li>A foreign election poster</li><li>A company handbook</li><li>An anonymous message</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The local constitutional text supplies the institutional framework.</p></details><p><strong>What is the focus of judicial review?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Legality under applicable superior law</li><li>Whether a judge likes a minister</li><li>How many people agree online</li><li>A private marketing campaign</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Review concerns lawful authority and limits.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish an appeal from political disagreement with a judgment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Review must follow an authorised legal route</li><li>Disagreement automatically cancels judgments</li><li>Only newspapers can review courts</li><li>No legal review exists anywhere</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Institutional disagreement does not create review powers.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Judicial review and Appeal.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draw a Liberian institutions chart with constitutional article references and write 500 words on the fictional departmental reversal.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M3:Fundamental Rights and Due Process in Liberia": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 3</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: FUNDAMENTAL RIGHTS AND DUE PROCESS IN LIBERIA</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constitutional Law</td><td>Fundamental Rights and Due Process in Liberia</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain fundamental rights and due process in liberia using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A fundamental right protects an interest recognised by the Constitution. Due process concerns legally required fairness in decisions affecting protected interests. A rights analysis identifies the holder, state action, protected interest, restriction and available legal response.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Due process</td><td>Required legal fairness in the decision-making process</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>The substantive question whether a claim is justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read the full article rather than a slogan about freedom. Identify whether the text protects persons, citizens or another specified group. Notice exceptions and linked procedures. Analyse equality using the treatment being compared and the justification offered. A right to a fair hearing does not predetermine the hearing result. Constitutional protections should guide procedure even when an allegation is unpopular.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a public institution removes a student from its programme after an accusation without explaining the case or offering a response. Identify the decision and the student interest, obtain the governing rules, and assess the constitutional and procedural protections. Separate whether misconduct occurred from whether the process was fair. A remedy for procedural failure may involve a lawful reconsideration rather than automatic permanent reinstatement.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Articles 11, 20 and 21 and record the rights, procedures and qualifications in the text.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both due process and merits. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A fundamental right protects an interest recognised by the Constitution. Due process concerns legally required fairness in decisions affecting protected interests. A rights analysis identifies the holder, state action, protected interest, restriction and available legal response.</li><li>Separate due process from merits.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Due process</td><td>Required legal fairness in the decision-making process</td></tr><tr><td>Merits</td><td>The substantive question whether a claim is justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Due process</summary><p>Required legal fairness in the decision-making process</p></details><details><summary>Merits</summary><p>The substantive question whether a claim is justified</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does a fair hearing guarantee that the claimant wins?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, it concerns fair process</li><li>Yes, in every case</li><li>Only if the claimant is famous</li><li>It eliminates factual disputes</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Fair procedure and substantive entitlement are separate questions.</p></details><p><strong>Before saying a right was violated, what should be identified?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The protected interest and challenged action</li><li>Only a political slogan</li><li>Only the claimant age</li><li>Only foreign case names</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A legal claim needs an applicable right and facts.</p></details><p><strong>Why read the whole constitutional article?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Qualifications and procedures can change its application</li><li>Every article says the same thing</li><li>Headlines replace the text</li><li>Exceptions are never relevant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Partial reading can distort scope.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Due process and Merits.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a rights-analysis table for the fictional student removal. Quote no more than a short phrase; paraphrase and cite the relevant article.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M4:Civil Jurisdiction, Venue and Service of Process": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 4</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CIVIL JURISDICTION, VENUE AND SERVICE OF PROCESS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil Procedure</td><td>Civil Jurisdiction, Venue and Service of Process</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain civil jurisdiction, venue and service of process using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Jurisdiction is a court legal authority over a matter or person. Venue concerns the authorised location for proceedings. Service of process gives notice by the legally required method. These requirements are related but are not interchangeable.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>Legal competence of a court</td></tr><tr><td>Venue</td><td>Legally proper place for the proceeding</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Before drafting a claim, identify the court competence, the parties and how notice must be given. A meritorious claim can encounter procedural barriers. Make a separate checklist for subject matter, parties, venue and service. Foreign federal jurisdiction rules in a video illustrate distinctions only; do not transplant dollar thresholds or filing routes into Liberia. For real proceedings, use the applicable statute, current rules and professional advice.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Paynesville supplier files an invoice claim and sends the defendant only an informal text. The defendant lives in another county. Investigate court competence and venue, then the authorised service method. The text may show actual awareness but does not by itself prove legally sufficient service. Keep proof of each procedural step in a dated record.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Chapters 3 and 4 and check the applicable Judiciary Law and current court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both jurisdiction and venue. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Jurisdiction is a court legal authority over a matter or person. Venue concerns the authorised location for proceedings. Service of process gives notice by the legally required method. These requirements are related but are not interchangeable.</li><li>Separate jurisdiction from venue.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>Legal competence of a court</td></tr><tr><td>Venue</td><td>Legally proper place for the proceeding</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Jurisdiction</summary><p>Legal competence of a court</p></details><details><summary>Venue</summary><p>Legally proper place for the proceeding</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which concept concerns court authority over the type of claim?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Subject-matter jurisdiction</li><li>Venue alone</li><li>The claimant preference</li><li>Court decoration</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Competence over the matter differs from location.</p></details><p><strong>What does venue principally concern?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The legally proper place for proceedings</li><li>The truth of testimony</li><li>The damages amount alone</li><li>The lawyer nationality</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Venue addresses geographical placement under law.</p></details><p><strong>Why retain proof of service?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Notice must be shown through the authorised process</li><li>It proves every allegation</li><li>It replaces a judgment</li><li>It is a tax receipt</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Service is an independent procedural requirement.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Jurisdiction and Venue.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Build a four-part filing checklist for the invoice scenario. Cite the chapter you would consult for each question; do not invent filing deadlines.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M4:Pleadings, Disclosure, Judgment and Civil Appeals": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 4</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PLEADINGS, DISCLOSURE, JUDGMENT AND CIVIL APPEALS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civil Procedure</td><td>Pleadings, Disclosure, Judgment and Civil Appeals</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain pleadings, disclosure, judgment and civil appeals using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Pleadings state claims and responses. Disclosure concerns obtaining relevant information through lawful procedure. A judgment decides an issue or case. An appeal asks an authorised reviewing court to examine alleged errors under the applicable process.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pleading</td><td>Formal statement of a claim or response</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Material offered to establish disputed facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>A complaint should organise parties, material facts, legal grounds and relief. An answer separates admissions, denials and defences. Disclosure is not permission to take private records without authority. After judgment, identify the reviewable issue, relevant record and procedural route. Deadlines and required steps must be researched directly; a foreign video or general course note is not a safe filing calendar.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: the supplier alleges nonpayment; the customer has a receipt but no delivery record. In a mock answer, admit the agreement if true, dispute nonpayment with the receipt, and identify whether delivery is contested. Plan requests for the invoice, payment ledger and delivery acknowledgement. On appeal, identify an actual alleged legal error instead of repeating that the result feels unfair.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Chapters 9, 13 and 51 together with the current rules for the relevant court.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both pleading and evidence. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Pleadings state claims and responses. Disclosure concerns obtaining relevant information through lawful procedure. A judgment decides an issue or case. An appeal asks an authorised reviewing court to examine alleged errors under the applicable process.</li><li>Separate pleading from evidence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pleading</td><td>Formal statement of a claim or response</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Material offered to establish disputed facts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Pleading</summary><p>Formal statement of a claim or response</p></details><details><summary>Evidence</summary><p>Material offered to establish disputed facts</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What is the principal job of an answer?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Respond to allegations and state applicable defences</li><li>Automatically erase the complaint</li><li>Replace witness testimony</li><li>Publish private records</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An answer joins and clarifies the disputed issues.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate pleading from evidence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An allegation is not itself proof</li><li>Every allegation proves itself</li><li>Receipts are pleadings only</li><li>Evidence is never relevant</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A party must support contested facts with admissible material.</p></details><p><strong>A mock appeal says only “the decision is unfair”. What is missing?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An identified error tied to the record and law</li><li>A brighter cover page</li><li>The judge home address</li><li>A social media petition</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Appellate analysis needs a legally relevant ground.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Pleading and Evidence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft an educational complaint outline, answer outline and disclosure list for the supplier dispute. Mark all documents “fictional classroom exercise”.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M5:Contract Formation and Enforceability": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 5</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONTRACT FORMATION AND ENFORCEABILITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Contracts</td><td>Contract Formation and Enforceability</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain contract formation and enforceability using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A contract is an agreement that the applicable law will enforce. Formation analysis distinguishes an offer, acceptance, the exchange or consideration where required, capacity, legality and any formal requirements. A conversation about a possible deal is not necessarily a completed agreement.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Offer</td><td>Proposal sufficiently definite to invite acceptance</td></tr><tr><td>Negotiation</td><td>Discussion that may not yet create binding obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read words and conduct objectively in context. Identify the parties, goods or services, price, timing and acceptance method. Consider whether a message is an invitation to negotiate rather than an offer. A changed term can require further agreement. Do not assume every oral agreement is unenforceable or that a signature cures illegality. Check the local law applicable to the particular transaction.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Monrovia printer quotes $200 for 500 booklets; the buyer replies “I accept at $150”. The changed price raises a counteroffer issue, not clear acceptance of the original terms. A later message agreeing $150 may establish agreement. Assemble the whole exchange and payment evidence. Distinguish proof of negotiation from proof of final assent.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Study the breach-of-contract pleading form in Appendix I; find Liberian judgments on formation through the Judiciary.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both offer and negotiation. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A contract is an agreement that the applicable law will enforce. Formation analysis distinguishes an offer, acceptance, the exchange or consideration where required, capacity, legality and any formal requirements. A conversation about a possible deal is not necessarily a completed agreement.</li><li>Separate offer from negotiation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Offer</td><td>Proposal sufficiently definite to invite acceptance</td></tr><tr><td>Negotiation</td><td>Discussion that may not yet create binding obligations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Offer</summary><p>Proposal sufficiently definite to invite acceptance</p></details><details><summary>Negotiation</summary><p>Discussion that may not yet create binding obligations</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>A buyer accepts only at a different price. What issue arises?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether this is a counteroffer rather than acceptance</li><li>Automatic acceptance of every term</li><li>Automatic criminal guilt</li><li>No need for evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A changed essential term can prevent matching assent.</p></details><p><strong>Why identify capacity in formation analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Some parties may lack legally sufficient ability to contract</li><li>Capacity means the contract page size</li><li>All adults must refuse agreements</li><li>It replaces agreement</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Enforceability can depend on legal capacity.</p></details><p><strong>Does a signature alone make an unlawful bargain enforceable?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, legality must still be checked</li><li>Yes, always</li><li>Only if written in blue</li><li>Only if printed twice</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Formal execution does not remove legal prohibitions.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Offer and Negotiation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce a formation timeline from six fictional messages and write an IRAC answer on the changed-price reply.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M5:Contract Breach, Defences and Remedies": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 5</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CONTRACT BREACH, DEFENCES AND REMEDIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Contracts</td><td>Contract Breach, Defences and Remedies</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain contract breach, defences and remedies using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A breach is failure to perform an enforceable contractual obligation without a legally sufficient excuse. Remedies respond to established liability; damages, restitution, rescission and specific performance serve different purposes and depend on applicable law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Damages</td><td>Money awarded for legally recoverable loss</td></tr><tr><td>Specific performance</td><td>A court-directed performance remedy where legally available</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Analyse formation before breach. State the obligation, performance date and actual conduct, then examine any variation, payment, prevention or recognised excuse. Link loss to the breach and explain evidence. Mitigation asks what reasonable steps could limit loss, not whether the injured party must accept any substitute. Avoid treating every disappointing transaction as fraud or promising a particular remedy without local authority.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a tailor agrees to supply uniforms for $300; late delivery makes a documented $40 replacement expense necessary. Distinguish recovery of money paid from compensation for additional loss. A claim for $10,000 based only on anger lacks a reasoned evidential basis. If the buyer accepted a new deadline, analyse that change before calling the delivery late.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate contract-remedy cases and compare the civil-law forms for damages and specific performance.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both damages and specific performance. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A breach is failure to perform an enforceable contractual obligation without a legally sufficient excuse. Remedies respond to established liability; damages, restitution, rescission and specific performance serve different purposes and depend on applicable law.</li><li>Separate damages from specific performance.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Damages</td><td>Money awarded for legally recoverable loss</td></tr><tr><td>Specific performance</td><td>A court-directed performance remedy where legally available</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Damages</summary><p>Money awarded for legally recoverable loss</p></details><details><summary>Specific performance</summary><p>A court-directed performance remedy where legally available</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What should be established before alleging a contractual breach?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An enforceable obligation and its terms</li><li>Only disappointment</li><li>Only a verbal accusation</li><li>Only a large loss estimate</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> There must first be an obligation capable of breach.</p></details><p><strong>Which remedy aims at carrying out an obligation rather than only paying money?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Specific performance</li><li>An invoice reminder</li><li>A newspaper notice</li><li>A private apology</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Specific performance concerns ordered performance where available.</p></details><p><strong>Why document replacement expenses?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>To connect claimed loss to the breach</li><li>To guarantee any amount requested</li><li>To prove every defence fails</li><li>To avoid checking terms</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Remedy analysis depends on causation and evidence.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Damages and Specific performance.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a loss schedule with supporting evidence and two possible remedies for the uniform scenario. Explain one defence and its effect.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M6:Negligence: Duty, Breach, Causation and Loss": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 6</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NEGLIGENCE: DUTY, BREACH, CAUSATION AND LOSS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Torts</td><td>Negligence: Duty, Breach, Causation and Loss</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain negligence: duty, breach, causation and loss using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Negligence is a civil wrong involving failure to meet an applicable standard of reasonable care. Analyse duty, breach, causation and legally recoverable damage separately. An accident alone does not establish every element.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Breach of duty</td><td>Failure to meet the applicable care standard</td></tr><tr><td>Causation</td><td>The legally relevant connection between conduct and loss</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Duty asks whether the defendant owed the relevant obligation. Breach compares conduct with the standard in the circumstances, without judging solely through hindsight. Factual causation asks whether the breach caused the loss; legal causation concerns the limits of responsibility. List evidence for each element. Defences and the treatment of claimant fault depend on applicable Liberian law; do not import a foreign percentage rule.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop leaves a leaking container beside its entrance; a customer slips and incurs a verified clinic expense. Identify who controlled the entrance, whether the danger was known or reasonably discoverable, and whether the spill caused the fall. A witness who saw the customer trip elsewhere changes causation. A good answer tests competing explanations before proposing compensation.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Study the negligence pleading form in Appendix I and locate a Liberian negligence judgment.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both breach of duty and causation. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Negligence is a civil wrong involving failure to meet an applicable standard of reasonable care. Analyse duty, breach, causation and legally recoverable damage separately. An accident alone does not establish every element.</li><li>Separate breach of duty from causation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Breach of duty</td><td>Failure to meet the applicable care standard</td></tr><tr><td>Causation</td><td>The legally relevant connection between conduct and loss</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Breach of duty</summary><p>Failure to meet the applicable care standard</p></details><details><summary>Causation</summary><p>The legally relevant connection between conduct and loss</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which statement best describes an accident in negligence analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It requires an element-by-element investigation</li><li>It automatically proves liability</li><li>It proves intention</li><li>It eliminates causation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An accident is a fact, not a complete legal test.</p></details><p><strong>A spill existed but did not cause the fall. Which element is especially problematic?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Causation</li><li>The shop name</li><li>The claimant citizenship alone</li><li>The paper size</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A breach must connect to the claimed loss.</p></details><p><strong>What does the reasonable-care comparison assess?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Conduct in the relevant circumstances</li><li>Only the final injury severity</li><li>Only how wealthy the defendant is</li><li>Only popularity</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The standard concerns what reasonable care required at the time.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Breach of duty and Causation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create an element-by-element evidence table for the shop accident and a 600-word balanced analysis including claimant fault.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M6:Intentional Torts and Civil Defences": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 6</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INTENTIONAL TORTS AND CIVIL DEFENCES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Torts</td><td>Intentional Torts and Civil Defences</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain intentional torts and civil defences using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An intentional tort concerns deliberate conduct meeting the elements of a civil wrong. Civil liability and criminal liability are separate inquiries even when they arise from the same event. Consent and lawful justification are possible issues, with limits set by applicable law.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tort</td><td>Civil wrong for which law may provide a remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Legally relevant answer to an otherwise established claim</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the particular wrong rather than calling every harmful act negligence. For contact, restraint or property interference, ask what was done, with what intention, and with what lawful authority. Consent has a scope: agreeing to one act does not automatically authorise another. A defence must be supported by facts and a legal rule. Remedies require a separate assessment of harm and available relief.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop guard locks a visitor inside after a payment argument. Investigate restraint, duration, consent and authority, and distinguish a civil claim from any criminal allegation. A store policy alone does not prove authority to detain. Do not physically test detention powers for an assignment; work only with fictional facts and lawful research.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Consult Appendix I forms on assault, battery, false imprisonment, fraud and trespass; research relevant local decisions.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both tort and defence. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An intentional tort concerns deliberate conduct meeting the elements of a civil wrong. Civil liability and criminal liability are separate inquiries even when they arise from the same event. Consent and lawful justification are possible issues, with limits set by applicable law.</li><li>Separate tort from defence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tort</td><td>Civil wrong for which law may provide a remedy</td></tr><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Legally relevant answer to an otherwise established claim</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Tort</summary><p>Civil wrong for which law may provide a remedy</p></details><details><summary>Defence</summary><p>Legally relevant answer to an otherwise established claim</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Can one event raise both civil and criminal issues?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Yes, the legal tests and remedies must be separated</li><li>No event can ever do so</li><li>Civil claims always replace prosecution</li><li>Criminal charges automatically award damages</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The same facts can engage distinct legal processes.</p></details><p><strong>A visitor agrees to a bag check. What must be assessed before relying on consent to detention?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The scope of the actual consent</li><li>Only the guard job title</li><li>Only the shop policy font</li><li>The visitor favourite colour</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Consent to one act does not necessarily cover another.</p></details><p><strong>What is wrong with treating a store policy as sufficient detention authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A private policy does not by itself supply lawful power</li><li>Policies are always criminal laws</li><li>Every policy is a court judgment</li><li>No law applies in shops</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Authority must come from applicable law and facts.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Tort and Defence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a mock case note on the guard scenario, analysing the alleged wrong, one defence, evidence needed and possible remedy.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M7:Criminal Offences, Conduct and Mental Elements": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 7</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CRIMINAL OFFENCES, CONDUCT AND MENTAL ELEMENTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Law</td><td>Criminal Offences, Conduct and Mental Elements</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain criminal offences, conduct and mental elements using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A criminal offence is conduct prohibited by penal law with legally specified elements and consequences. Actus reus describes required conduct, circumstances or results; mens rea describes the required mental state where the offence requires one.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actus reus</td><td>External elements of an offence</td></tr><tr><td>Mens rea</td><td>Required mental element, where specified by law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with the offence text, not a moral judgement. Break it into elements and identify what evidence addresses each. Intention, knowledge, recklessness and negligence are different ideas. Do not assume every offence has the same mental requirement. Omission liability needs an applicable duty. A person accusation, arrest or social media confession must still be analysed through the governing evidential and procedural rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a worker carries away a bag believing it is their own. An analysis of a theft allegation must examine ownership, taking, knowledge or intent as required by the actual offence. A genuine mistake can matter, but saying “mistake” is not proof. The answer should identify what the worker saw, what the bag contained and how the claim could be tested.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the general principles in Title 26 and a selected offence provision; check subsequent amendments.</p><p><a href=\"https://assets.tobaccocontrollaws.org/uploads/legislation/Liberia/Liberia-Penal-Law-native.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Penal Law, Title 26 — primary statute reproduction (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both actus reus and mens rea. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A criminal offence is conduct prohibited by penal law with legally specified elements and consequences. Actus reus describes required conduct, circumstances or results; mens rea describes the required mental state where the offence requires one.</li><li>Separate actus reus from mens rea.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actus reus</td><td>External elements of an offence</td></tr><tr><td>Mens rea</td><td>Required mental element, where specified by law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Actus reus</summary><p>External elements of an offence</p></details><details><summary>Mens rea</summary><p>Required mental element, where specified by law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why start criminal analysis with the offence provision?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It specifies the elements to be proved</li><li>It guarantees the accused is guilty</li><li>It replaces all evidence</li><li>It eliminates defence rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Criminal liability depends on the applicable legal definition.</p></details><p><strong>Which term describes a required mental element?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Mens rea</li><li>Venue</li><li>Probate</li><li>Consideration</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Mental state is distinct from external conduct.</p></details><p><strong>An accused took the wrong bag honestly. What issue needs analysis?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the required mental element is established</li><li>Whether a bag has a handle</li><li>Whether all mistakes prove guilt</li><li>Whether civil filing fees were paid</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Mistake may affect the mental requirement under the offence.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Actus reus and Mens rea.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Select one offence in the Penal Law and prepare an elements table with fictional evidence for and against each element.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M7:Criminal Defences, Participation and Sentencing": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 7</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CRIMINAL DEFENCES, PARTICIPATION AND SENTENCING</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Law</td><td>Criminal Defences, Participation and Sentencing</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain criminal defences, participation and sentencing using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A criminal defence can negate an element or provide a legally recognised justification or excuse. Participation concerns liability for involvement in another person conduct. Sentencing follows conviction and applies legally available penalties and relevant factors.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Argument affecting whether liability is established</td></tr><tr><td>Mitigation</td><td>Factors relevant to a lawful penalty after conviction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Do not confuse innocence with mitigation: mitigation addresses consequences after liability, while a defence may prevent liability. Analyse the precise role of each person in a group. Presence alone and active assistance are different facts, but the governing participation rule must be checked. For a justification, identify its conditions and limits. Never assume that revenge or a private instruction creates lawful authority.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: three people are nearby during damage to a stall; one breaks the lock, one allegedly encourages it, and one only watches. Separate their conduct and evidence rather than treating the group as a single defendant. If one claims protection from an immediate threat, analyse necessity and the applicable justification conditions. Punishment cannot be invented to match public anger.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read general responsibility and justification provisions and the penalty for the selected offence; verify amendments.</p><p><a href=\"https://assets.tobaccocontrollaws.org/uploads/legislation/Liberia/Liberia-Penal-Law-native.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Penal Law, Title 26 — primary statute reproduction (check amendments)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both defence and mitigation. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A criminal defence can negate an element or provide a legally recognised justification or excuse. Participation concerns liability for involvement in another person conduct. Sentencing follows conviction and applies legally available penalties and relevant factors.</li><li>Separate defence from mitigation.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Argument affecting whether liability is established</td></tr><tr><td>Mitigation</td><td>Factors relevant to a lawful penalty after conviction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Defence</summary><p>Argument affecting whether liability is established</p></details><details><summary>Mitigation</summary><p>Factors relevant to a lawful penalty after conviction</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which distinction separates a defence from mitigation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Liability versus factors affecting penalty</li><li>Page numbering versus font</li><li>Civil court versus printing</li><li>Only the accused wealth</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Defences and mitigation answer different legal questions.</p></details><p><strong>Why analyse each group member separately?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Their conduct and mental involvement may differ</li><li>Everyone nearby is automatically guilty</li><li>Groups cannot be investigated</li><li>Witnesses become defendants automatically</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Individual responsibility requires individual evidence.</p></details><p><strong>What limits a proposed criminal sentence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The applicable law and lawful sentencing process</li><li>Only public anger</li><li>Only a private agreement</li><li>Only a newspaper poll</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Legal punishment must have a legal foundation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Defence and Mitigation.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare three separate liability analyses for the stall scenario and a sentencing-research checklist without recommending an unverified penalty.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M8:Arrest, Detention, Bail and Access to Counsel": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 8</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ARREST, DETENTION, BAIL AND ACCESS TO COUNSEL</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Procedure</td><td>Arrest, Detention, Bail and Access to Counsel</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain arrest, detention, bail and access to counsel using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Arrest brings a person under legal restraint. Detention is continuing custody. Bail concerns conditional release under applicable law; it is not a judgment of innocence or guilt. Constitutional protections and criminal procedure must be read together.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bail</td><td>Conditional release under a lawful process</td></tr><tr><td>Conviction</td><td>Determination of criminal guilt through the required process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>For a detention timeline, record the basis, arrest time, information given, access to counsel, court appearance and orders. Identify the competent authority and the applicable safeguards. Article 21 contains important protections, but read its full qualifications before stating an absolute rule. Never substitute a foreign bail amount or timetable for Liberian law. Use authorised counsel and court processes for actual cases.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a learner hears that a relative was arrested but has only a forwarded voice note. Separate confirmed information from rumour and prepare neutral questions about the legal basis, location, counsel and court order. Do not publish accusations or obstruct officers. A classroom memo should state what the record establishes and what cannot yet be determined.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Article 21 alongside the current Criminal Procedure Law and relevant court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both bail and conviction. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Arrest brings a person under legal restraint. Detention is continuing custody. Bail concerns conditional release under applicable law; it is not a judgment of innocence or guilt. Constitutional protections and criminal procedure must be read together.</li><li>Separate bail from conviction.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bail</td><td>Conditional release under a lawful process</td></tr><tr><td>Conviction</td><td>Determination of criminal guilt through the required process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Bail</summary><p>Conditional release under a lawful process</p></details><details><summary>Conviction</summary><p>Determination of criminal guilt through the required process</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does release on bail determine innocence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, release and guilt are separate decisions</li><li>Yes in every case</li><li>Only for rich defendants</li><li>It cancels the offence provision</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Bail addresses custody, not a final guilt determination.</p></details><p><strong>Which record helps analyse detention safeguards?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A verified chronology and legal basis</li><li>Only an anonymous voice note</li><li>Only a family rumour</li><li>Only the officer nickname</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Time and authority are central to procedural analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Why read constitutional detention qualifications carefully?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The legal protection may contain specific conditions</li><li>A headline is more binding</li><li>All qualifications are optional</li><li>Foreign rules automatically apply</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The full provision controls its scope.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Bail and Conviction.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a fictional detention timeline and a safeguards checklist citing Article 21. Explain why a bail decision does not decide the accusation.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M8:Criminal Trials, Proof and Fair-Hearing Safeguards": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 8</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CRIMINAL TRIALS, PROOF AND FAIR-HEARING SAFEGUARDS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Criminal Procedure</td><td>Criminal Trials, Proof and Fair-Hearing Safeguards</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain criminal trials, proof and fair-hearing safeguards using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A criminal trial is a legally regulated process for determining a charge. The presumption of innocence and prosecution burden require proof through the applicable process. A charge is an allegation, not a conviction.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charge</td><td>Formal accusation to be determined</td></tr><tr><td>Presumption of innocence</td><td>Protection against treating accusation alone as established guilt</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Map accusation, plea, evidence, defence response, adjudication and any lawful review. Examine each element of the charge against the evidence. A defendant silence must be analysed under the constitutional protection, not treated as a confession. Assess witness reliability without assuming that confident delivery proves truth. A fair hearing requires both institutional safeguards and meaningful opportunity to respond.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an online crowd declares a trader guilty because the police arrested them. Explain the missing legal steps and the difference between suspicion and proof. If two witnesses disagree, identify what each could observe, the source of knowledge and corroborating records. A model answer should analyse evidence and the burden rather than count supportive comments.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Article 21 and the relevant trial provisions of the Criminal Procedure Law; compare with the current court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both charge and presumption of innocence. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A criminal trial is a legally regulated process for determining a charge. The presumption of innocence and prosecution burden require proof through the applicable process. A charge is an allegation, not a conviction.</li><li>Separate charge from presumption of innocence.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charge</td><td>Formal accusation to be determined</td></tr><tr><td>Presumption of innocence</td><td>Protection against treating accusation alone as established guilt</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Charge</summary><p>Formal accusation to be determined</p></details><details><summary>Presumption of innocence</summary><p>Protection against treating accusation alone as established guilt</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does an arrest by itself establish about guilt?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It does not replace a lawful determination of the charge</li><li>It proves every element</li><li>It is the final verdict</li><li>It eliminates defence rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Restraint and adjudication are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>How should conflicting witnesses be assessed?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Examine knowledge, reliability and corroboration</li><li>Choose the louder witness</li><li>Count social media followers</li><li>Ignore all testimony</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Reliability depends on evidence, not performance alone.</p></details><p><strong>Which distinction is central to criminal proof?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Suspicion versus proof of the required elements</li><li>Formal shoes versus sandals</li><li>County size versus population</li><li>Ink colour versus paper</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A suspicion may justify inquiry without establishing guilt.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Charge and Presumption of innocence.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a fictional trial-stage diagram and a 500-word explanation correcting the crowd claim, with constitutional references.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M9:Relevance, Burdens and Standards of Proof": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 9</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: RELEVANCE, BURDENS AND STANDARDS OF PROOF</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Relevance, Burdens and Standards of Proof</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain relevance, burdens and standards of proof using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Relevance concerns whether evidence bears on a legally material fact. Admissibility asks whether the applicable rules permit it. Weight concerns persuasive value. The burden identifies who must prove what; the standard identifies the required degree of proof.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Admissibility</td><td>Whether evidence may legally be received</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>How persuasive the admitted material is</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin with the disputed legal element and explain why an item matters. A relevant item can still face an exclusion rule. An admissible item can carry little weight. Standards vary by proceeding and issue; do not apply a criminal standard mechanically to every civil question. Distinguish the burden of producing evidence from the ultimate burden of persuasion when the governing law does so.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a claimant proves the defendant owns an expensive car but alleges an unpaid invoice. Wealth may have no direct connection to whether this invoice was paid. A bank transfer record linked to the invoice is more informative. Explain the link, then test authenticity and whether another payment could account for the record. Do not treat volume of documents as proof quality.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Chapter 25 on evidence and consult Liberian decisions on proof in the relevant type of proceeding.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both admissibility and weight. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Relevance concerns whether evidence bears on a legally material fact. Admissibility asks whether the applicable rules permit it. Weight concerns persuasive value. The burden identifies who must prove what; the standard identifies the required degree of proof.</li><li>Separate admissibility from weight.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Admissibility</td><td>Whether evidence may legally be received</td></tr><tr><td>Weight</td><td>How persuasive the admitted material is</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Admissibility</summary><p>Whether evidence may legally be received</p></details><details><summary>Weight</summary><p>How persuasive the admitted material is</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Can relevant evidence still be inadmissible?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Yes, another applicable rule may exclude it</li><li>No, relevance guarantees admission</li><li>Only if it is short</li><li>Only if it is printed</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Relevance is one part of admissibility.</p></details><p><strong>What does evidential weight concern?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Persuasive value</li><li>Document kilograms</li><li>The filing fee</li><li>The witness nationality alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Weight describes strength in proving a point.</p></details><p><strong>A defendant expensive car is offered to prove nonpayment. What must be explained?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its connection to a material disputed fact</li><li>Its colour alone</li><li>Its brand popularity</li><li>The claimant favourite model</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Evidence needs a logical connection to the issue.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Admissibility and Weight.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Classify ten fictional items by relevance, admissibility issues and likely weight for the invoice dispute.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M9:Hearsay, Documents, Digital Evidence and Authentication": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 9</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: HEARSAY, DOCUMENTS, DIGITAL EVIDENCE AND AUTHENTICATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Hearsay, Documents, Digital Evidence and Authentication</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain hearsay, documents, digital evidence and authentication using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Hearsay analysis asks whether an out-of-court statement is being used to prove what it asserts under the governing evidence rules. Authentication concerns showing an item is what its proponent claims. A screenshot content and its provenance are separate issues.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Showing an item is what it is claimed to be</td></tr><tr><td>Provenance</td><td>The recorded origin and history of material</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>State the purpose for which a statement is offered. The same words can raise different issues if offered as notice instead of truth. Then check the local rule and any exception. For documents, preserve originals, source, dates and lawful access. For digital material, record the account, export method and continuity. A printout does not automatically prove who created or sent it.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: “My neighbour said the vendor received payment” is offered to prove payment. Identify the original speaker and truth-purpose issue, then seek first-hand or documentary support. A cropped transfer screenshot also needs account and transaction verification. Do not edit images to make them appear clearer evidence; keep the original and distinguish any annotated classroom copy.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the applicable evidence provisions and find local decisions on documentary proof; compare rather than import foreign hearsay rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both authentication and provenance. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Hearsay analysis asks whether an out-of-court statement is being used to prove what it asserts under the governing evidence rules. Authentication concerns showing an item is what its proponent claims. A screenshot content and its provenance are separate issues.</li><li>Separate authentication from provenance.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Authentication</td><td>Showing an item is what it is claimed to be</td></tr><tr><td>Provenance</td><td>The recorded origin and history of material</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Authentication</summary><p>Showing an item is what it is claimed to be</p></details><details><summary>Provenance</summary><p>The recorded origin and history of material</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why identify the purpose of an out-of-court statement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The evidential use affects the hearsay analysis</li><li>All statements are automatically excluded</li><li>Purpose never matters</li><li>It determines the witness salary</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Truth and other purposes may be treated differently under the rule.</p></details><p><strong>Does a printed screenshot prove its author automatically?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, authentication remains necessary</li><li>Yes, every time</li><li>Only in colour</li><li>Only on expensive paper</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Format does not establish provenance.</p></details><p><strong>Which practice best preserves digital evidence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Retain the original and record lawful source and handling</li><li>Overwrite the file with edits</li><li>Delete all context</li><li>Change the timestamp</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Integrity and traceability help verification.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Authentication and Provenance.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare an evidence-preservation log and a hearsay-purpose analysis using fictional messages; include two reliability limitations.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M10:Land Categories and Customary Land Rights in Liberia": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 10</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LAND CATEGORIES AND CUSTOMARY LAND RIGHTS IN LIBERIA</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Property and Land</td><td>Land Categories and Customary Land Rights in Liberia</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain land categories and customary land rights in liberia using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The Land Rights Act distinguishes private, customary, government and public land. Land classification helps identify ownership, management and transaction requirements. Customary land should not simply be treated as ownerless land because no individual deed is produced.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customary land</td><td>Land rights recognised through the statutory customary-land framework</td></tr><tr><td>Public land</td><td>A distinct statutory category; not a synonym for all rural land</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the parcel and claimed category before discussing transfer. Separate ownership from occupation and management authority. Community land analysis needs evidence of the community, boundaries, recognised rights and lawful decision process. Do not assume a chief signature alone settles every community consent issue. Record women and other affected members as participants in the analysis, not as invisible background.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an investor is offered a rural parcel by one person who claims authority for the whole community. Request the land classification, boundary evidence and legal basis for representation. Compare the proposed process with the Act. A promise of jobs is not itself proof of valid consent or title. A balanced memo identifies both development interests and unresolved rights.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Land Rights Act definitions and provisions on categories and customary land; check implementation measures with the LLA.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both customary land and public land. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The Land Rights Act distinguishes private, customary, government and public land. Land classification helps identify ownership, management and transaction requirements. Customary land should not simply be treated as ownerless land because no individual deed is produced.</li><li>Separate customary land from public land.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Customary land</td><td>Land rights recognised through the statutory customary-land framework</td></tr><tr><td>Public land</td><td>A distinct statutory category; not a synonym for all rural land</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Customary land</summary><p>Land rights recognised through the statutory customary-land framework</p></details><details><summary>Public land</summary><p>A distinct statutory category; not a synonym for all rural land</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Which classification belongs to the Land Rights Act framework?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Customary land</li><li>Social-media land</li><li>Unlimited land</li><li>Foreign-video land</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Act recognises customary land as a distinct category.</p></details><p><strong>Does absence of an individual deed prove community land is ownerless?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, customary rights require analysis</li><li>Yes in every situation</li><li>Only in rural counties</li><li>Only when an investor asks</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Documentation and rights must be assessed under the Act.</p></details><p><strong>Why check authority before accepting one signature for a community?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Representation and consent must have a lawful basis</li><li>A signature always binds every resident</li><li>Only the pen brand matters</li><li>Communities cannot have rights</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A transaction needs competent authority and required participation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Customary land and Public land.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a fictional parcel-classification and community-stakeholder table. Cite the exact provisions found in the Act for each entry.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M10:Deeds, Leases, Boundaries and Property Due Diligence": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 10</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DEEDS, LEASES, BOUNDARIES AND PROPERTY DUE DILIGENCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Property and Land</td><td>Deeds, Leases, Boundaries and Property Due Diligence</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain deeds, leases, boundaries and property due diligence using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Due diligence is structured investigation before a transaction. A deed records a transfer claim; a lease grants specified use or possession for a term. Boundaries identify the parcel, while title analysis asks whether the seller can lawfully convey the claimed interest.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deed</td><td>Instrument documenting a claimed conveyance</td></tr><tr><td>Lease</td><td>Grant of specified possession or use for a term</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Check seller identity and authority, the chain of title, parcel description, occupation, encumbrances and competing claims. A survey does not by itself resolve every ownership issue. Registration, execution and tax questions need the current legal process. A lease should identify premises, term, rent, permitted use, repairs and lawful termination arrangements. Explain uncertainty before anyone relies on the transaction.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: two sellers present documents for the same Paynesville lot. Compare grantor, dates, description and transaction history rather than choosing the newer photocopy automatically. Inspect only through lawful arrangements and use competent professionals for real title work. In a fictional lease exercise, distinguish the landlord ownership claim from the tenant right to occupy for a fixed term.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read transfer provisions in the Land Rights Act and examine applicable real-property procedures and current LLA guidance.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=79:land-rights-act&amp;start=40\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Land Rights Act 2018 — Liberia Land Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both deed and lease. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Due diligence is structured investigation before a transaction. A deed records a transfer claim; a lease grants specified use or possession for a term. Boundaries identify the parcel, while title analysis asks whether the seller can lawfully convey the claimed interest.</li><li>Separate deed from lease.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deed</td><td>Instrument documenting a claimed conveyance</td></tr><tr><td>Lease</td><td>Grant of specified possession or use for a term</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Deed</summary><p>Instrument documenting a claimed conveyance</p></details><details><summary>Lease</summary><p>Grant of specified possession or use for a term</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does a survey principally help establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The parcel location and boundaries</li><li>Automatic unchallengeable title</li><li>All tax exemptions</li><li>The seller identity by itself</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Boundary evidence and ownership authority are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>Which item belongs in title due diligence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The seller authority and chain of title</li><li>Only the purchase price</li><li>Only the seller confidence</li><li>Only a photograph of a fence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A seller must be able to convey the relevant interest.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes a lease from full ownership transfer?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A lease grants a defined use or possession interest for a term</li><li>A lease never has obligations</li><li>A lease is always a criminal charge</li><li>A lease proves every title claim</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The interest conveyed depends on the instrument and law.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Deed and Lease.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional title-risk register and a one-page lease term sheet with five unresolved due-diligence questions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M11:Marriage, Family Obligations and Child Protection": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 11</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: MARRIAGE, FAMILY OBLIGATIONS AND CHILD PROTECTION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Family Law and Estates</td><td>Marriage, Family Obligations and Child Protection</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain marriage, family obligations and child protection using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Family law regulates legal relationships and obligations involving spouses, children and other family members. Marriage status, support, custody and protection are separate questions. A family disagreement does not remove a child independent interests.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Support</td><td>Legally relevant provision for a dependent needs</td></tr><tr><td>Custody</td><td>Authority and arrangements concerning a child care under applicable law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the form of relationship, legal recognition, parties and issue. Do not assume one marriage rule applies to every statutory or customary setting. In child matters, examine applicable protection and welfare requirements, evidence of care, and competent authority. Avoid unsupported age or inheritance rules from old summaries. Distinguish family support from ownership of all household assets. Use confidential fictional facts in assignments.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: separated parents dispute school expenses and where their child should live. List the child needs, caregiving arrangements, relevant records and legal questions without declaring one parent entitled merely by gender. If violence is alleged, safety and appropriate professional referral come before a classroom negotiation plan. The learning exercise is legal analysis, not an instruction to handle a real child case alone.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Domestic Relations Law sections relevant to marriage and support; also consult the Children’s Law and current amendments through the Ministry of Justice.</p><p><a href=\"https://lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=59:domestic-relations-law&amp;start=20\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Domestic Relations Law, Title 9 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both support and custody. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Family law regulates legal relationships and obligations involving spouses, children and other family members. Marriage status, support, custody and protection are separate questions. A family disagreement does not remove a child independent interests.</li><li>Separate support from custody.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Support</td><td>Legally relevant provision for a dependent needs</td></tr><tr><td>Custody</td><td>Authority and arrangements concerning a child care under applicable law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Support</summary><p>Legally relevant provision for a dependent needs</p></details><details><summary>Custody</summary><p>Authority and arrangements concerning a child care under applicable law</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why separate child support from custody?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They concern different obligations and arrangements</li><li>Support automatically decides every custody issue</li><li>Children have no separate interests</li><li>Custody is only a property sale</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Related family questions still require distinct analysis.</p></details><p><strong>Which approach to family-law research is sound?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Identify the recognised relationship and applicable law</li><li>Assume all marriage systems are identical</li><li>Use only a foreign video</li><li>Ignore later amendments</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Relationship type and governing law matter.</p></details><p><strong>What belongs first in an exercise involving alleged family violence?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Safety and appropriate professional referral</li><li>Forced reconciliation</li><li>Publication of private names</li><li>A competition about blame</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Safety concerns should not be reduced to classroom bargaining.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Support and Custody.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional family-law issue map and a neutral information checklist, including child welfare and confidentiality.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M11:Decedents’ Estates, Probate and Trust Fundamentals": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 11</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DECEDENTS’ ESTATES, PROBATE AND TRUST FUNDAMENTALS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Family Law and Estates</td><td>Decedents’ Estates, Probate and Trust Fundamentals</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain decedents’ estates, probate and trust fundamentals using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An estate comprises rights and obligations left by a deceased person. Probate and estate administration concern lawful authority to manage succession. A will directs distribution subject to legal requirements; a trust involves holding or administering assets for specified purposes or beneficiaries.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Probate</td><td>Court-related process concerning wills and estate administration</td></tr><tr><td>Intestacy</td><td>Succession where an effective will does not govern the property</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify assets, debts, claimed beneficiaries, documents and the proper court. Distinguish a proposed personal representative from someone lawfully authorised. A will must satisfy applicable validity requirements and may not dispose of rights the deceased never owned. Intestacy means no effective will governs the relevant property; it does not mean any relative may seize it. Do not import foreign tax exemptions or automatic trust advantages.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a family member withdraws estate money because they found an unsigned note. Investigate authority, the note status, debts and relevant succession law. Prepare an inventory and preserve records. A fair classroom answer distinguishes the family relationship from a legal appointment and explains why distributions should not be assumed before the estate obligations are identified.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Consult the current Monthly and Probate Court rules and locate the applicable Decedents Estates Law; distinguish statutory and customary issues.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both probate and intestacy. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>An estate comprises rights and obligations left by a deceased person. Probate and estate administration concern lawful authority to manage succession. A will directs distribution subject to legal requirements; a trust involves holding or administering assets for specified purposes or beneficiaries.</li><li>Separate probate from intestacy.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Probate</td><td>Court-related process concerning wills and estate administration</td></tr><tr><td>Intestacy</td><td>Succession where an effective will does not govern the property</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Probate</summary><p>Court-related process concerning wills and estate administration</p></details><details><summary>Intestacy</summary><p>Succession where an effective will does not govern the property</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does intestacy describe?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Succession without an effective governing will</li><li>Automatic government ownership of all property</li><li>A criminal sentence</li><li>A land survey</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its effect depends on applicable succession law.</p></details><p><strong>Why identify estate debts before distribution?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Obligations affect what can lawfully be distributed</li><li>Debts disappear automatically at death</li><li>Only beneficiaries can create debts</li><li>An inventory is unnecessary</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Estate administration concerns obligations as well as assets.</p></details><p><strong>Does family relationship alone prove authority to administer an estate?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, lawful authority must be established</li><li>Yes in every case</li><li>Only if the relative is eldest</li><li>Only if the family agrees informally</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Appointment and applicable legal procedures require verification.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Probate and Intestacy.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Build a fictional estate inventory, debt list and authority checklist; compare a will and trust without claiming automatic tax or probate outcomes.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M12:Decent Work: Employment Terms and Workplace Rights": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 12</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: DECENT WORK: EMPLOYMENT TERMS AND WORKPLACE RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Labour Law</td><td>Decent Work: Employment Terms and Workplace Rights</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain decent work: employment terms and workplace rights using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Employment law regulates the relationship between worker and employer under its applicable scope. A contract records agreed terms, while legislation can establish protections that cannot simply be removed by a private agreement.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employment terms</td><td>Agreed and legally required conditions of work</td></tr><tr><td>Contractor label</td><td>Description that must be tested against the actual relationship</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Begin by determining the Act applicability rather than assuming every public or private arrangement is identical. Distinguish an employee from an independent contractor by the actual relationship and applicable test, not the label alone. Keep records of work, remuneration, hours, leave, safety and instructions. Research current wage rules directly; this course does not prescribe a static rate that could become outdated.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a business labels all staff “volunteers” but sets full working hours and pays regular amounts. Investigate the relationship before accepting the label. Record supervision, duties and payment terms, then compare the statutory definitions and protections. A worker signature on a form is evidence, but not conclusive proof that every statutory protection can be waived.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Decent Work Act introductory provisions and relevant employment-contract and protection sections; verify its application to the worker.</p><p><a href=\"https://csa.gov.lr/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Decent-Work-Act-2015.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decent Work Act 2015 — Civil Service Agency</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both employment terms and contractor label. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Employment law regulates the relationship between worker and employer under its applicable scope. A contract records agreed terms, while legislation can establish protections that cannot simply be removed by a private agreement.</li><li>Separate employment terms from contractor label.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Employment terms</td><td>Agreed and legally required conditions of work</td></tr><tr><td>Contractor label</td><td>Description that must be tested against the actual relationship</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Employment terms</summary><p>Agreed and legally required conditions of work</p></details><details><summary>Contractor label</summary><p>Description that must be tested against the actual relationship</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why examine the real work relationship rather than only its label?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Legal classification may depend on substance</li><li>Every label is conclusive</li><li>Only the company name matters</li><li>Payment never matters</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The governing test concerns actual circumstances.</p></details><p><strong>Can a private employment agreement automatically remove every statutory protection?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, applicable mandatory protections must be checked</li><li>Yes always</li><li>Only if signed twice</li><li>Only if written abroad</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Contract terms are subject to applicable law.</p></details><p><strong>Where should a current minimum wage rule be checked?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Current official law and guidance</li><li>An undated foreign video</li><li>A guessed amount</li><li>A social-media comment</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Wage requirements can change and need authoritative verification.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Employment terms and Contractor label.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional employment term sheet and a statute-applicability memo; identify wage and leave provisions to verify, without inventing rates.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M12:Termination, Workplace Disputes and Labour Remedies": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 12</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TERMINATION, WORKPLACE DISPUTES AND LABOUR REMEDIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Labour Law</td><td>Termination, Workplace Disputes and Labour Remedies</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain termination, workplace disputes and labour remedies using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Termination ends an employment relationship, but the legal basis, notice, procedure and consequences require separate analysis. A workplace complaint may follow a specialised process rather than an ordinary civil route.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Termination ground</td><td>The reason asserted for ending employment</td></tr><tr><td>Procedure</td><td>The lawful steps for reaching and challenging the decision</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Distinguish misconduct, capability, redundancy, resignation and expiry of a defined term. Identify evidence and the applicable procedure before evaluating fairness. An allegation is different from a substantiated ground. Keep a chronology of notices, responses, payments and referrals. A proposed remedy should follow the local law and competent forum; do not promise automatic reinstatement or fixed compensation from a foreign example.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a worker is dismissed after being absent, but had submitted medical documentation. Examine the reason, records, employer response and statutory procedure. If the alleged absence is disputed, a timeline and attendance register matter more than assumptions. In a learning memo, list remedy options to research and state what evidence could change your conclusion.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read relevant termination and dispute-resolution sections of the Decent Work Act and current National Labour Court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://csa.gov.lr/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/Decent-Work-Act-2015.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Decent Work Act 2015 — Civil Service Agency</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both termination ground and procedure. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Termination ends an employment relationship, but the legal basis, notice, procedure and consequences require separate analysis. A workplace complaint may follow a specialised process rather than an ordinary civil route.</li><li>Separate termination ground from procedure.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Termination ground</td><td>The reason asserted for ending employment</td></tr><tr><td>Procedure</td><td>The lawful steps for reaching and challenging the decision</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Termination ground</summary><p>The reason asserted for ending employment</p></details><details><summary>Procedure</summary><p>The lawful steps for reaching and challenging the decision</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why analyse both dismissal grounds and process?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A reason and lawful decision procedure are distinct</li><li>A stated reason makes procedure irrelevant</li><li>Procedure alone proves every allegation</li><li>All dismissals are identical</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Legal fairness may depend on both dimensions.</p></details><p><strong>A worker has a medical record contradicting an absence allegation. What should be done?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Compare it with the timeline and governing rules</li><li>Destroy the record</li><li>Ignore it because an allegation exists</li><li>Assume guaranteed compensation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Relevant contrary evidence must be assessed.</p></details><p><strong>What must be checked before choosing a labour-dispute forum?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The applicable specialised statutory process</li><li>Only the nearest building</li><li>Only the worker preference</li><li>Only a foreign court name</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Competence and procedural route come from law.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Termination ground and Procedure.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional dismissal chronology, evidence list and forum-research note addressing both substantive grounds and procedure.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M13:Corporations, Separate Personality and Governance": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 13</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: CORPORATIONS, SEPARATE PERSONALITY AND GOVERNANCE</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Corporations, Agency and Partnership</td><td>Corporations, Separate Personality and Governance</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain corporations, separate personality and governance using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A corporation recognised by law can have a legal identity separate from its members. Limited liability and separate personality are related but distinct. Governance allocates powers and responsibilities among owners, directors and officers.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Separate personality</td><td>Entity identity distinct from members</td></tr><tr><td>Governance</td><td>Rules allocating decision powers and duties</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the entity type, incorporation documents and governing statute. Directors must act within their powers and applicable duties; a shareholder vote does not automatically cure every defect. Keep business and personal assets and decisions appropriately recorded. Legal personality does not guarantee immunity for fraud or personal wrongdoing. For a Liberian enterprise, verify the registration and reporting regime applicable to its actual operations.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop company signs a supply contract, but the founder is asked to pay personally. Examine the named contracting party, any personal guarantee and relevant exceptions before assuming the founder is liable. Conversely, a company account is not permission for an officer to take money for personal use. Separate ownership, control, representation and responsibility.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the Associations Law and amendments in the Registry library; distinguish domestic business registration and applicable local compliance requirements.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both separate personality and governance. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A corporation recognised by law can have a legal identity separate from its members. Limited liability and separate personality are related but distinct. Governance allocates powers and responsibilities among owners, directors and officers.</li><li>Separate separate personality from governance.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Separate personality</td><td>Entity identity distinct from members</td></tr><tr><td>Governance</td><td>Rules allocating decision powers and duties</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Separate personality</summary><p>Entity identity distinct from members</p></details><details><summary>Governance</summary><p>Rules allocating decision powers and duties</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What does separate corporate personality mean?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The recognised entity has its own legal identity</li><li>Every owner is immune from all wrongdoing</li><li>The company cannot enter contracts</li><li>Directors have no duties</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Entity identity and personal immunity are different concepts.</p></details><p><strong>Why check the contracting party name?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It helps identify whose obligation was created</li><li>Names never matter</li><li>Every founder signs personally</li><li>Only the logo determines liability</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The agreement and authority inform responsibility.</p></details><p><strong>What can change an owner exposure on a company debt?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A valid personal guarantee or applicable exception</li><li>Only the company colour</li><li>Only the office rent</li><li>Only the owner friendship</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Liability needs specific legal and factual grounds.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Separate personality and Governance.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional company governance chart and compare company debt with a founder personal guarantee.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M13:Agency Authority, Partnerships and Fiduciary Duties": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 13</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: AGENCY AUTHORITY, PARTNERSHIPS AND FIDUCIARY DUTIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Corporations, Agency and Partnership</td><td>Agency Authority, Partnerships and Fiduciary Duties</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain agency authority, partnerships and fiduciary duties using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Agency involves one person acting on behalf of another with legally relevant authority. Actual authority comes from the principal authorisation; apparent authority concerns representations attributable to the principal under the applicable test. A partnership is a distinct business relationship requiring its own analysis.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actual authority</td><td>Authority arising from the principal authorisation</td></tr><tr><td>Apparent authority</td><td>Authority assessed through relevant outward representations and reliance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify principal, agent, third party, representation and scope. A person claiming to be authorised is not always enough; examine what the principal communicated or permitted. Internal restrictions and third-party knowledge may affect liability. Partnership obligations depend on the legal form and agreement. Fiduciary duties concern acting appropriately for another interest, including conflicts and improper benefit where law imposes those duties.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a procurement officer orders equipment above an internal limit. The supplier has received prior authorised orders from that officer. Compare actual authority, outward representations and what the supplier knew. Do not assume an internal memo automatically defeats a third-party claim. State which facts support each side and which local rule must be located.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the applicable Associations Law provisions and Liberian cases on agency; compare foreign examples with local authority.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both actual authority and apparent authority. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Agency involves one person acting on behalf of another with legally relevant authority. Actual authority comes from the principal authorisation; apparent authority concerns representations attributable to the principal under the applicable test. A partnership is a distinct business relationship requiring its own analysis.</li><li>Separate actual authority from apparent authority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actual authority</td><td>Authority arising from the principal authorisation</td></tr><tr><td>Apparent authority</td><td>Authority assessed through relevant outward representations and reliance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Actual authority</summary><p>Authority arising from the principal authorisation</p></details><details><summary>Apparent authority</summary><p>Authority assessed through relevant outward representations and reliance</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What is central to actual authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The principal authorisation</li><li>The agent confidence alone</li><li>A supplier wish</li><li>A social media title</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Actual authority derives from the principal-agent relationship.</p></details><p><strong>Whose representations are especially important to apparent authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Those attributable to the principal under the legal test</li><li>Only an agent unsupported boast</li><li>Only a stranger rumour</li><li>Only a buyer preference</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Apparent authority requires legally relevant manifestations.</p></details><p><strong>Why check a supplier knowledge of an internal limit?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It may affect the authority and reliance analysis</li><li>Knowledge never matters</li><li>It makes every purchase void</li><li>It replaces the contract</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The facts may change whether the principal is bound.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Actual authority and Apparent authority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write an agency authority memo for the equipment purchase and a short partnership term sheet addressing decisions, contributions and disputes.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M14:Negotiable Instruments and Commercial Payment Risk": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 14</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NEGOTIABLE INSTRUMENTS AND COMMERCIAL PAYMENT RISK</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Commercial Payments and Security</td><td>Negotiable Instruments and Commercial Payment Risk</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain negotiable instruments and commercial payment risk using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A negotiable instrument is a payment document meeting statutory requirements for transfer and enforcement. A promise to pay, an order to pay, an ordinary invoice and an electronic transfer are not necessarily the same legal instrument.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Promise to pay</td><td>Undertaking by its maker</td></tr><tr><td>Order to pay</td><td>Direction to another person or institution to pay</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify maker, drawer, drawee, payee and holder where relevant. Analyse form, amount, payment terms, transfer and signature using the applicable Commercial Code. A document label does not establish negotiability. Dishonour, forged signatures and third-party claims require specific legal treatment. A foreign Uniform Commercial Code video explains concepts but does not automatically state Liberia rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a trader writes “I will pay if my goods sell” and calls it a negotiable note. The condition creates a statutory-form question. Compare the actual requirements rather than assuming the title controls. For a cheque, separate issuance, presentment, payment and any dishonour. Preserve records and do not promise that possession always defeats earlier claims.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Locate the Commercial Code provisions on payment instruments; use the Central Bank as a starting point for the current framework.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.registry.cbl.org.lr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Collateral Registry — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both promise to pay and order to pay. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A negotiable instrument is a payment document meeting statutory requirements for transfer and enforcement. A promise to pay, an order to pay, an ordinary invoice and an electronic transfer are not necessarily the same legal instrument.</li><li>Separate promise to pay from order to pay.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Promise to pay</td><td>Undertaking by its maker</td></tr><tr><td>Order to pay</td><td>Direction to another person or institution to pay</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Promise to pay</summary><p>Undertaking by its maker</p></details><details><summary>Order to pay</summary><p>Direction to another person or institution to pay</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does calling a document negotiable make it legally negotiable?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, statutory requirements must be satisfied</li><li>Yes automatically</li><li>Only if printed in capital letters</li><li>Only if signed twice</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The law assesses its substance and form.</p></details><p><strong>What distinguishes a payment promise from an order?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>One undertakes payment; the other directs payment</li><li>Both always transfer land</li><li>Neither names parties</li><li>Only an order can be written</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Their roles and liabilities may differ.</p></details><p><strong>Why examine a condition attached to payment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It may affect negotiability under the applicable law</li><li>Conditions never matter</li><li>It always proves fraud</li><li>It automatically clears debt</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The statutory requirements need close comparison.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Promise to pay and Order to pay.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Annotate three fictional payment documents and identify the statutory questions affecting transfer and enforcement.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M14:Secured Transactions, Collateral and Priority": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 14</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: SECURED TRANSACTIONS, COLLATERAL AND PRIORITY</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Commercial Payments and Security</td><td>Secured Transactions, Collateral and Priority</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain secured transactions, collateral and priority using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A secured transaction gives a creditor a legally recognised interest in collateral to support an obligation. Creation of the interest, effectiveness against others, priority and enforcement are different questions. A registry entry is not proof that every underlying agreement is valid.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collateral</td><td>Asset supporting a secured obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Priority</td><td>Ranking of competing legally recognised claims</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify debtor, secured creditor, obligation and described asset. Check the agreement and legally required steps. Search for prior interests and analyse priority under the statute rather than assuming possession or registration always wins. Distinguish movable collateral from land-security regimes. Default does not authorise threats or unlimited self-help; enforcement must follow the applicable law.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: two lenders each claim a shop generator as collateral. Build a chronology of agreements, asset descriptions and registration. The first payment received is not automatically the priority rule. Examine whether each security interest arose and the applicable effectiveness and priority provisions. A careful conclusion states assumptions and unresolved registry information.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Central Bank Collateral Registry explanation of Chapter 5 of the Commercial Code and locate the governing text.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.registry.cbl.org.lr/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Collateral Registry — Central Bank of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both collateral and priority. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A secured transaction gives a creditor a legally recognised interest in collateral to support an obligation. Creation of the interest, effectiveness against others, priority and enforcement are different questions. A registry entry is not proof that every underlying agreement is valid.</li><li>Separate collateral from priority.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Collateral</td><td>Asset supporting a secured obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Priority</td><td>Ranking of competing legally recognised claims</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Collateral</summary><p>Asset supporting a secured obligation</p></details><details><summary>Priority</summary><p>Ranking of competing legally recognised claims</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What is collateral?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An asset supporting an obligation</li><li>A court nickname</li><li>A witness statement</li><li>A company slogan</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Security concerns an asset and the supported obligation.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish creation from priority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An interest can exist while its rank against others needs separate analysis</li><li>Creation always decides every priority issue</li><li>Priority means the debt is cancelled</li><li>Registration replaces all contracts</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The legal questions are related but distinct.</p></details><p><strong>What helps analyse two claims over a generator?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Agreement and registration chronology plus the statutory rules</li><li>Only creditor wealth</li><li>Only generator colour</li><li>Only debtor promises</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Priority depends on the applicable rule and relevant events.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Collateral and Priority.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional two-creditor priority table and lawful-enforcement research checklist. Do not use real debtor records.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M15:Administrative Powers and Review of Public Decisions": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 15</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ADMINISTRATIVE POWERS AND REVIEW OF PUBLIC DECISIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administrative Law, Legislation and Tax</td><td>Administrative Powers and Review of Public Decisions</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain administrative powers and review of public decisions using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Administrative law concerns how public bodies exercise legal powers. Review asks whether a decision was authorised and reached through the applicable legal process. A merits appeal, where provided, can have a different scope from legality review.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Discretion</td><td>Choice within legally authorised limits</td></tr><tr><td>Review</td><td>Examination through an authorised legal mechanism</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Start with the enabling law, decision-maker, facts, procedure and reasons. Ask whether the body considered the legally relevant matters and respected required participation. A discretionary power is not an unlimited power. Identify the remedy and competent forum separately. Use foreign judicial-review tests comparatively; a Liberia answer needs local authority rather than a transplanted foreign statute.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: an agency refuses a licence because of an unpublished requirement unrelated to its governing law. Obtain the application, decision and rule before analysing power and procedure. A court challenge should identify a legal defect, not merely dissatisfaction. If a statutory review route exists, research its conditions and timing before choosing another route.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Identify the relevant constitutional protections, agency enabling Act and available review procedure in the current court rules.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/constitution-of-liberia-2/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">1986 Constitution — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both discretion and review. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Administrative law concerns how public bodies exercise legal powers. Review asks whether a decision was authorised and reached through the applicable legal process. A merits appeal, where provided, can have a different scope from legality review.</li><li>Separate discretion from review.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Discretion</td><td>Choice within legally authorised limits</td></tr><tr><td>Review</td><td>Examination through an authorised legal mechanism</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Discretion</summary><p>Choice within legally authorised limits</p></details><details><summary>Review</summary><p>Examination through an authorised legal mechanism</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What should be located first when analysing an agency power?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The enabling legal provision</li><li>The agency slogan</li><li>A foreign politician statement</li><li>A staff personal preference</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Public authority must have a lawful foundation.</p></details><p><strong>Does discretion mean unlimited authority?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, it operates within legal limits</li><li>Yes always</li><li>Only during busy periods</li><li>Only when a licence is expensive</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Choice remains constrained by law.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish merits review from legality review?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They may examine different questions and remedies</li><li>Both always retry every fact</li><li>Neither needs authority</li><li>Only one has paperwork</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The available review scope depends on the legal route.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Discretion and Review.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional licence-decision review memo covering power, process, reasons, forum and missing facts.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M15:Legislation, Interpretation and Tax-Law Research": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 15</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LEGISLATION, INTERPRETATION AND TAX-LAW RESEARCH</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Administrative Law, Legislation and Tax</td><td>Legislation, Interpretation and Tax-Law Research</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain legislation, interpretation and tax-law research using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Legislation is law made through the authorised legislative process. Interpretation determines meaning in context. Tax analysis identifies the taxable event, person, base, rate, period and governing amendment; rates must be researched for the relevant date.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bill</td><td>Proposed legislation awaiting the required process</td></tr><tr><td>Commencement</td><td>When a legal provision starts to operate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read definitions, scope, exceptions and cross-references before applying a single sentence. Distinguish a bill from an enacted law and commencement from publication date. An amendment may alter part of a scheme without replacing all of it. For tax, verify the relevant period and current official guidance. Never quote a memorised rate for a real liability without checking whether it remains applicable.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a business relies on an old training slide for a tax calculation after the law has changed. Compare the transaction date and commencement provisions, then reconstruct the applicable rule. A drafting exercise should specify who must do what, when, and with what exceptions. Ambiguous definitions create disputes even when the policy goal is clear.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the LRA current laws-and-amendments page and read an amendment together with the provision it changes.</p><p><a href=\"https://revenue.lra.gov.lr/tax-laws-and-amendments/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Tax laws and amendments — Liberia Revenue Authority</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both bill and commencement. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Legislation is law made through the authorised legislative process. Interpretation determines meaning in context. Tax analysis identifies the taxable event, person, base, rate, period and governing amendment; rates must be researched for the relevant date.</li><li>Separate bill from commencement.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bill</td><td>Proposed legislation awaiting the required process</td></tr><tr><td>Commencement</td><td>When a legal provision starts to operate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Bill</summary><p>Proposed legislation awaiting the required process</p></details><details><summary>Commencement</summary><p>When a legal provision starts to operate</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why distinguish a bill from an enacted Act?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A proposal is not automatically operative law</li><li>Every proposal is already binding</li><li>Bills can never become law</li><li>Only the title matters</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Legal effect depends on the authorised process.</p></details><p><strong>What should be checked before applying a tax rate?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The relevant date and current governing provisions</li><li>Only an old training slide</li><li>Only a competitor guess</li><li>Only a foreign tax website</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Time and amendments determine the applicable rule.</p></details><p><strong>Why read statutory definitions first?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They can give terms a specific legal meaning</li><li>Definitions are always optional</li><li>They replace every exception</li><li>They are never used in law</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Context and defined terms shape interpretation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Bill and Commencement.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a mock amendment-reading table and a 300-word plain-language clause regulating a fictional permit; include definitions and commencement.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M16:Human Rights Instruments and Domestic Remedies": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 16</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: HUMAN RIGHTS INSTRUMENTS AND DOMESTIC REMEDIES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Law</td><td>Human Rights Instruments and Domestic Remedies</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain human rights instruments and domestic remedies using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Human rights are protections associated with human dignity. An international declaration, treaty, constitutional provision and court remedy have different legal functions. A persuasive moral claim still needs the applicable legal framework when presented as a legal claim.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Declaration</td><td>Formal statement of principles, distinct from a treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Legally available response to an established wrong</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the protected right, duty bearer, alleged interference and remedy. A treaty status question requires ratification and entry-into-force checks; domestic enforceability requires separate local analysis. Do not assume every international body can hear every complaint. For documentation, use accurate facts and protect identifying information. Community advocacy is stronger when the requested action names a responsible actor and a realistic legal basis.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a public service excludes people with a disability. Compare the relevant constitutional protection, applicable legislation and international instruments. Separate the evidence of exclusion from legal interpretation. A classroom recommendation can request accessible procedures and lawful review while recording what must still be researched. Avoid promising that an international complaint automatically reverses a local decision.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the UDHR and identify corresponding protections in Liberia Constitution; distinguish declarations from binding treaty obligations.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.un.org/en/about-us/universal-declaration-of-human-rights\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Universal Declaration of Human Rights — United Nations</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both declaration and remedy. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Human rights are protections associated with human dignity. An international declaration, treaty, constitutional provision and court remedy have different legal functions. A persuasive moral claim still needs the applicable legal framework when presented as a legal claim.</li><li>Separate declaration from remedy.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Declaration</td><td>Formal statement of principles, distinct from a treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Legally available response to an established wrong</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Declaration</summary><p>Formal statement of principles, distinct from a treaty</p></details><details><summary>Remedy</summary><p>Legally available response to an established wrong</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Why distinguish a declaration from a treaty?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Their legal forms and effects differ</li><li>Every declaration requires ratification</li><li>Treaties are never binding</li><li>Both are court verdicts</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The instrument type affects the legal analysis.</p></details><p><strong>What should a rights memorandum identify?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Right, duty bearer, facts and remedy</li><li>Only emotional language</li><li>Only a photograph</li><li>Only an organisation name</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A claim needs a legal and factual structure.</p></details><p><strong>Does every international body accept every individual complaint?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, competence and admissibility must be checked</li><li>Yes automatically</li><li>Only if a post goes viral</li><li>Only if written in capitals</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Complaint mechanisms have specific conditions.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Declaration and Remedy.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional rights memorandum with a domestic-law section, an international-reference section and a remedy-research table.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M16:Treaties, International Law and Cross-Border Disputes": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 16</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TREATIES, INTERNATIONAL LAW AND CROSS-BORDER DISPUTES</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Law</td><td>Treaties, International Law and Cross-Border Disputes</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain treaties, international law and cross-border disputes using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Public international law regulates relations among states and other recognised international actors. A treaty is an international agreement governed by international law. Private international law addresses cross-border issues such as jurisdiction, applicable law and recognition of judgments.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Law used to decide the substantive issue</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction clause</td><td>Provision addressing the forum for disputes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Ask three separate questions in a cross-border contract: which court may hear the matter, which law governs, and whether a resulting judgment can be recognised elsewhere. A choice-of-law clause is not necessarily a choice of court. For treaties, check party status, reservations and dates rather than assuming signature alone settles every obligation. Analyse local implementation separately; do not announce a universal rule about all treaties in Liberia.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a Liberian exporter and a Ghanaian buyer use a contract selecting one country law but naming no forum. Separate governing law from court jurisdiction, then examine enforcement if assets are elsewhere. For a treaty assignment, record the actual Liberia status entry, relevant article and verification date. Do not substitute another country reservation for Liberia position.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the UN Treaty Collection to check one treaty status for Liberia. Read the instrument itself and distinguish international obligations from domestic application.</p><p><a href=\"https://treaties.un.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">United Nations Treaty Collection</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both governing law and jurisdiction clause. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Public international law regulates relations among states and other recognised international actors. A treaty is an international agreement governed by international law. Private international law addresses cross-border issues such as jurisdiction, applicable law and recognition of judgments.</li><li>Separate governing law from jurisdiction clause.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Governing law</td><td>Law used to decide the substantive issue</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction clause</td><td>Provision addressing the forum for disputes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Governing law</summary><p>Law used to decide the substantive issue</p></details><details><summary>Jurisdiction clause</summary><p>Provision addressing the forum for disputes</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Is a choice-of-law clause necessarily a choice of court?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, they answer different questions</li><li>Yes in every agreement</li><li>Only if typed</li><li>Only for exports</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Applicable law and adjudicative forum are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>What must be checked before claiming Liberia is bound by a treaty?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its status, dates and relevant reservations</li><li>Only the treaty title</li><li>Only another country signature</li><li>Only an online slogan</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Party status and instrument conditions determine obligations.</p></details><p><strong>Which question concerns use of a foreign judgment in another country?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Recognition and enforcement</li><li>Only contract formation</li><li>Only citizenship</li><li>Only an office licence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A judgment may require a separate enforcement analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Governing law and Jurisdiction clause.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a treaty-status research sheet and a fictional cross-border contract issues memo covering forum, applicable law and enforcement.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M17:Liberian Maritime Law and Shipping Transactions": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 17</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: LIBERIAN MARITIME LAW AND SHIPPING TRANSACTIONS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maritime and Insurance Law</td><td>Liberian Maritime Law and Shipping Transactions</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain liberian maritime law and shipping transactions using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Maritime law regulates shipping-related relationships and obligations. Flag-state authority, port-state authority, ship ownership and contractual responsibility are different concepts. Liberia-specific analysis begins with its Maritime Law and applicable international instruments.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flag state</td><td>State of vessel registration with relevant regulatory responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier</td><td>Party undertaking carriage under the applicable arrangement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify vessel, flag, location, parties and transaction. Distinguish shipowner, operator, carrier and cargo owner. A bill of lading, charterparty and insurance policy do not serve identical functions. Determine which law and forum govern the dispute and what convention provisions may apply. A United States video statement that a rule is federal describes that jurisdiction, not Liberia.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: cargo destined for Monrovia is damaged aboard a foreign-operated Liberian-flag vessel. Gather transport documents, the damage record, notice information and insurance details. Do not assume the flag alone answers every liability question. Prepare separate issues for carriage duties, governing law, forum and insurance recovery, with deadlines identified for professional verification.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Find the Maritime Law and relevant current notices in the Liberian Registry library; identify the version and any applicable convention.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both flag state and carrier. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Maritime law regulates shipping-related relationships and obligations. Flag-state authority, port-state authority, ship ownership and contractual responsibility are different concepts. Liberia-specific analysis begins with its Maritime Law and applicable international instruments.</li><li>Separate flag state from carrier.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flag state</td><td>State of vessel registration with relevant regulatory responsibilities</td></tr><tr><td>Carrier</td><td>Party undertaking carriage under the applicable arrangement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Flag state</summary><p>State of vessel registration with relevant regulatory responsibilities</p></details><details><summary>Carrier</summary><p>Party undertaking carriage under the applicable arrangement</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does a vessel flag automatically decide every cargo-liability issue?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, contracts and other applicable law must be analysed</li><li>Yes in every case</li><li>Only if the cargo is valuable</li><li>Only if the vessel is new</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Shipping disputes can involve multiple legal connections.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish carrier from shipowner?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The roles and contractual duties may differ</li><li>They are always identical</li><li>Neither can have obligations</li><li>Only names matter</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Responsibility follows the relevant role and law.</p></details><p><strong>What is wrong with applying a US federal maritime statement directly as Liberia law?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It skips the applicable Liberian and international framework</li><li>Every US rule binds Liberia</li><li>Maritime law has no jurisdiction</li><li>A video is a treaty</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Comparative material needs jurisdictional limits.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Flag state and Carrier.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional cargo-dispute document checklist and a one-page map distinguishing flag, contract, forum and insurance issues.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M17:Insurance Contracts, Coverage and Claims": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 17</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INSURANCE CONTRACTS, COVERAGE AND CLAIMS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Maritime and Insurance Law</td><td>Insurance Contracts, Coverage and Claims</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain insurance contracts, coverage and claims using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Insurance transfers specified risks under a contract. Coverage depends on the insured event, policy period, conditions, exclusions and applicable law. A loss and an insured loss are not always the same thing.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Risks and circumstances insured under the policy and law</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion</td><td>Provision limiting specified risks or circumstances</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Read the schedule, definitions, insuring clause, exclusions and claims conditions together. Identify policyholder, insured interest, insurer and beneficiary where relevant. Separate coverage from valuation of an accepted claim. Indemnity concerns compensation within the contract and law; subrogation can concern an insurer rights after payment. Do not assume every policy has identical disclosure or notification rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a shop flood occurs after a policy expires, while the owner assumes all previous insurance continues. Compare the dates, any renewal evidence and the coverage trigger. If an exclusion is invoked, read its actual wording and legal validity rather than accepting a verbal summary. A mock claim should accurately describe the event and attach genuine fictional records, without exaggeration.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Identify the applicable Liberian insurance regulatory requirements and the policy wording; research current regulator guidance before any real claim.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.liscr.com/onlinelibrary/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Liberian corporate and maritime legislation — Registry library</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both coverage and exclusion. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Insurance transfers specified risks under a contract. Coverage depends on the insured event, policy period, conditions, exclusions and applicable law. A loss and an insured loss are not always the same thing.</li><li>Separate coverage from exclusion.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Risks and circumstances insured under the policy and law</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion</td><td>Provision limiting specified risks or circumstances</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Coverage</summary><p>Risks and circumstances insured under the policy and law</p></details><details><summary>Exclusion</summary><p>Provision limiting specified risks or circumstances</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does every business loss fall within an insurance policy?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, coverage depends on terms and applicable law</li><li>Yes automatically</li><li>Only if reported online</li><li>Only if the owner is insured once</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Insurance covers specified risks and periods.</p></details><p><strong>Which documents should be read with an insuring clause?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Definitions, schedule, exclusions and conditions</li><li>Only advertising copy</li><li>Only the insurer logo</li><li>Only a bank receipt</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Policy provisions operate together.</p></details><p><strong>Why separate coverage from valuation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether a risk is insured differs from how much is payable</li><li>Valuation proves every exclusion invalid</li><li>Both always mean the premium</li><li>Neither needs evidence</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The two questions use related but distinct analysis.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Coverage and Exclusion.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Create a fictional insurance coverage matrix and claims letter. State the policy period, event, disputed term and missing evidence.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M18:Environmental Protection, Permits and Community Participation": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 18</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: ENVIRONMENTAL PROTECTION, PERMITS AND COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Environmental and Intellectual Property Law</td><td>Environmental Protection, Permits and Community Participation</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain environmental protection, permits and community participation using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Environmental law regulates activities affecting environmental resources and public welfare. An environmental impact assessment examines expected effects and alternatives within the applicable process. A permit is a legal authorisation with scope and conditions, not a general exemption from responsibility.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Impact assessment</td><td>Structured examination of proposed environmental effects</td></tr><tr><td>Permit condition</td><td>Legally relevant limit attached to authorisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the proposed activity, site, affected people, risks and competent authority. Separate a landowner permission from environmental approval. Read participation, assessment and compliance provisions together. Evidence can include lawful observations, maps and official records; do not enter dangerous or restricted sites for an assignment. Analyse prevention and remedies without presuming a particular project must be approved or refused.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a workshop proposes discharging waste near a stream used by residents. A private lease does not answer the environmental-permit question. Identify the potential impacts, legal authority, information needed and participation process. A useful compliance memo assigns actions and records rather than saying only “protect nature”. Include the possibility that missing baseline data prevents a final conclusion.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the EPA Environmental Protection and Management Law, especially the framework for assessment and permitting; check current regulations.</p><p><a href=\"https://epa.gov.lr/epa-documents/the-environmental-protection-and-management-law-of-liberia/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Environmental Protection and Management Law — EPA Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both impact assessment and permit condition. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Environmental law regulates activities affecting environmental resources and public welfare. An environmental impact assessment examines expected effects and alternatives within the applicable process. A permit is a legal authorisation with scope and conditions, not a general exemption from responsibility.</li><li>Separate impact assessment from permit condition.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Impact assessment</td><td>Structured examination of proposed environmental effects</td></tr><tr><td>Permit condition</td><td>Legally relevant limit attached to authorisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Impact assessment</summary><p>Structured examination of proposed environmental effects</p></details><details><summary>Permit condition</summary><p>Legally relevant limit attached to authorisation</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does a lease automatically supply environmental permission?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, environmental requirements need separate analysis</li><li>Yes everywhere</li><li>Only if rent is prepaid</li><li>Only if the site is rural</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Property permission and regulatory approval differ.</p></details><p><strong>What is a central purpose of environmental impact assessment?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Examine likely effects and alternatives under law</li><li>Guarantee approval</li><li>Replace every licence</li><li>Prove all risks are imaginary</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Assessment informs lawful decisions rather than predetermining them.</p></details><p><strong>Which fieldwork is appropriate for this course?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Safe fictional or publicly available information</li><li>Unauthorised entry into a hazardous site</li><li>Collecting chemicals without training</li><li>Confronting alleged polluters alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Learning tasks should not create physical or legal hazards.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Impact assessment and Permit condition.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a fictional workshop environmental-risk register with authority, evidence gaps, consultation steps and compliance actions.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M18:Intellectual Property, Copyright and Brand Protection": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 18</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY, COPYRIGHT AND BRAND PROTECTION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Environmental and Intellectual Property Law</td><td>Intellectual Property, Copyright and Brand Protection</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain intellectual property, copyright and brand protection using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Intellectual property concerns legally recognised interests in creations and identifiers. Copyright, trademarks and patents protect different subject matter. Owning a physical copy is different from holding rights to reproduce or commercially exploit protected material.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Copyright</td><td>Rights concerning qualifying creative expression</td></tr><tr><td>Trademark</td><td>Sign functioning to distinguish commercial source</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify the work or sign, author or rights holder, proposed use and applicable territorial law. A trademark distinguishes commercial origin; copyright concerns protected expression rather than every idea; patents concern qualifying inventions under the relevant system. Permission, licence and statutory exceptions require separate analysis. Attribution is not automatically a licence. Avoid assuming that content found online is free for commercial reuse.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a startup copies a photographer image and a competitor logo for its website. Separate copyright and trademark issues, ownership and the proposed use. Contacting a rights holder for permission differs from merely adding a credit line. A classroom audit should record asset source, licence scope, restrictions and evidence instead of claiming “no copyright intended” resolves the issue.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use WIPO to distinguish types of IP, then locate the applicable Liberian IP law and registration guidance before local application.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.wipo.int/en/web/about-ip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Intellectual property — WIPO</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both copyright and trademark. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Intellectual property concerns legally recognised interests in creations and identifiers. Copyright, trademarks and patents protect different subject matter. Owning a physical copy is different from holding rights to reproduce or commercially exploit protected material.</li><li>Separate copyright from trademark.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Copyright</td><td>Rights concerning qualifying creative expression</td></tr><tr><td>Trademark</td><td>Sign functioning to distinguish commercial source</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Copyright</summary><p>Rights concerning qualifying creative expression</p></details><details><summary>Trademark</summary><p>Sign functioning to distinguish commercial source</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Does crediting a creator automatically permit commercial reuse?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, permission or an applicable exception is still needed</li><li>Yes for every image</li><li>Only if the credit is large</li><li>Only if the site is new</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Attribution and authorisation are distinct.</p></details><p><strong>Which right principally concerns a sign identifying commercial source?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Trademark</li><li>Probate</li><li>Bail</li><li>Venue</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Trademarks concern source-identifying signs.</p></details><p><strong>Why distinguish a physical copy from copyright?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Possession does not automatically transfer exploitation rights</li><li>Buying a book transfers every right</li><li>Copies can never be owned</li><li>Copyright is always physical land</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Different interests can exist in the same item.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Copyright and Trademark.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a fictional website IP audit covering five assets, permission evidence, territorial questions and replacement options.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M19:Professional Ethics, Confidentiality and Conflicts": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 19</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: PROFESSIONAL ETHICS, CONFIDENTIALITY AND CONFLICTS</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Ethics and Trial Advocacy</td><td>Professional Ethics, Confidentiality and Conflicts</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain professional ethics, confidentiality and conflicts using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Legal ethics concerns duties governing professional conduct. Confidentiality limits disclosure of protected information; evidential privilege concerns legal protection against compelled disclosure under its applicable conditions. A conflict of interest can impair independent professional judgment.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Professional restriction on disclosure of protected information</td></tr><tr><td>Privilege</td><td>Legal protection against disclosure when its conditions apply</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify who the client is, the role undertaken and the relevant duties. Keep information secure and share only through lawful authority or valid permission. Consent does not automatically cure every conflict. A learner must not present this course certificate as a licence or take on reserved legal work without authorisation. For real issues, consult a properly authorised Liberian legal professional and the current rules.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a student helping a lawyer learns facts about a family dispute and is asked to post a success story online. Remove identifying information only after considering whether people could still be recognised, and obtain proper approval before any publication. If the office is approached by the opposing party, flag the potential conflict before receiving more confidential information. Do not promise that every conversation is privileged.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read the Judiciary Code of Moral and Professional Ethics and current professional rules; identify the rule before asserting an exception.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/code-of-moral-and-professional-ethic/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Code of Moral and Professional Ethics — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both confidentiality and privilege. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Legal ethics concerns duties governing professional conduct. Confidentiality limits disclosure of protected information; evidential privilege concerns legal protection against compelled disclosure under its applicable conditions. A conflict of interest can impair independent professional judgment.</li><li>Separate confidentiality from privilege.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confidentiality</td><td>Professional restriction on disclosure of protected information</td></tr><tr><td>Privilege</td><td>Legal protection against disclosure when its conditions apply</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Confidentiality</summary><p>Professional restriction on disclosure of protected information</p></details><details><summary>Privilege</summary><p>Legal protection against disclosure when its conditions apply</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Are professional confidentiality and evidential privilege identical?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, their scope and legal operation can differ</li><li>Yes in every situation</li><li>Neither concerns information</li><li>Both are court fees</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The governing rules must be analysed separately.</p></details><p><strong>What should happen when a potential opposing client contacts an office?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Screen the conflict before receiving unnecessary confidential details</li><li>Collect everything first</li><li>Publish the request</li><li>Guarantee representation</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Early screening reduces avoidable conflicts.</p></details><p><strong>What does a TIH course certificate establish?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Completion of this TIH educational course</li><li>Admission to the Liberian Bar</li><li>A law degree</li><li>A court appointment</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is not professional authorisation.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Confidentiality and Privilege.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Draft a fictional conflict-screening checklist and a secure information-handling plan. Explain the boundary between study and authorised practice.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M19:Trial Advocacy, Moot Court and Opinion Writing": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 19</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: TRIAL ADVOCACY, MOOT COURT AND OPINION WRITING</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal Ethics and Trial Advocacy</td><td>Trial Advocacy, Moot Court and Opinion Writing</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain trial advocacy, moot court and opinion writing using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Advocacy presents a supported legal position to a decision-maker. Moot court simulates legal argument for learning. An opinion explains the issues, applicable law, reasoning and result; it is different from a partisan submission.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Submission</td><td>A party supported argument and requested outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Opinion</td><td>Reasoned explanation by the decision-maker</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Build an issue list, record references, authorities and requested relief. In oral argument, answer the question asked before returning to the planned structure. Admit a factual or legal gap rather than inventing an authority. For witness exercises, distinguish open questions from leading assertions and follow applicable rules. A written opinion should address the strongest argument on each side and explain why one analysis prevails.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: counsel argues a land dispute and the bench asks where the seller authority appears in the record. A strong response supplies the exact fictional document reference or acknowledges that it is absent. Reading a prepared speech without answering weakens the argument. In the opinion exercise, state whether the missing authority prevents a final result and identify the lawful next step to research.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read current trial and Supreme Court rules. Use a published Liberian judgment for structure, not as a source of invented facts.</p><p><a href=\"https://judiciary.gov.lr/court-rules/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Court rules — Judiciary of Liberia</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both submission and opinion. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Advocacy presents a supported legal position to a decision-maker. Moot court simulates legal argument for learning. An opinion explains the issues, applicable law, reasoning and result; it is different from a partisan submission.</li><li>Separate submission from opinion.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Submission</td><td>A party supported argument and requested outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Opinion</td><td>Reasoned explanation by the decision-maker</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Submission</summary><p>A party supported argument and requested outcome</p></details><details><summary>Opinion</summary><p>Reasoned explanation by the decision-maker</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What should an advocate do when the bench asks a direct question?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Answer it accurately before returning to the structure</li><li>Ignore it and read faster</li><li>Invent a paragraph number</li><li>Ask social media to vote</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Responsive, candid argument supports credibility.</p></details><p><strong>How does a judicial opinion differ from a party submission?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It gives the decision-maker reasoned resolution</li><li>It must advocate only for one client</li><li>It replaces all evidence</li><li>It is always oral</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An opinion explains the adjudicative outcome.</p></details><p><strong>What is proper when a needed authority cannot be verified?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Acknowledge the gap and avoid inventing it</li><li>Create a convincing case name</li><li>Quote a nonexistent paragraph</li><li>Claim a secret source</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Candour is essential to legal work.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Submission and Opinion.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Prepare a five-minute moot submission and a 900-word fictional reasoned opinion. Include adverse authority and record references.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M20:Jurisprudence: Legal Positivism and Natural Law": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 20</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: JURISPRUDENCE: LEGAL POSITIVISM AND NATURAL LAW</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisprudence and Dispute Resolution</td><td>Jurisprudence: Legal Positivism and Natural Law</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain jurisprudence: legal positivism and natural law using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Jurisprudence studies the nature, authority and interpretation of law. Legal positivist approaches commonly distinguish legal validity from moral merit. Natural-law approaches give moral reasoning a foundational role in understanding law. These broad traditions contain disagreements and should not be reduced to slogans.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal validity</td><td>Whether a norm qualifies as law under the relevant criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Moral merit</td><td>Whether a norm is ethically justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Distinguish describing what counts as law from evaluating whether it is just. A person may criticise a law morally while acknowledging its institutional status. Conversely, moral conviction alone does not identify a valid procedure or remedy. In an essay, define the theory, explain its reasoning, present a counterargument and connect it to a specific problem. Do not attribute an official school endorsement to this independent TIH course.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a rule validly adopted on its face treats one community unfairly. Compare institutional-validity questions, constitutional limits and moral criticism. A weak essay says “all law is good” or “any unfair rule may be ignored”. A stronger essay explains how theories differ, checks superior-law limits and separates lawful reform or challenge from personal disagreement.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Use the published subject list as a study map, then compare theories against a fictional rule and the Liberian constitutional framework.</p><p><a href=\"https://liberiaschooloflaw.com/areas-of-study/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Published subject list — Liberia School of Law (reference only)</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both legal validity and moral merit. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Jurisprudence studies the nature, authority and interpretation of law. Legal positivist approaches commonly distinguish legal validity from moral merit. Natural-law approaches give moral reasoning a foundational role in understanding law. These broad traditions contain disagreements and should not be reduced to slogans.</li><li>Separate legal validity from moral merit.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal validity</td><td>Whether a norm qualifies as law under the relevant criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Moral merit</td><td>Whether a norm is ethically justified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Legal validity</summary><p>Whether a norm qualifies as law under the relevant criteria</p></details><details><summary>Moral merit</summary><p>Whether a norm is ethically justified</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>What distinction is central to many positivist accounts?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Legal validity versus moral merit</li><li>Paper versus ink</li><li>Only courts versus offices</li><li>Civil claims versus shipping</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A norm status and ethical evaluation can be distinguished.</p></details><p><strong>What is characteristic of natural-law approaches?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A foundational role for moral reasoning in understanding law</li><li>Complete rejection of ethical questions</li><li>The claim that every rule is a contract</li><li>A system of land surveying</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The tradition examines law in relation to moral principles.</p></details><p><strong>What improves a jurisprudence essay?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Defined concepts, reasons, counterarguments and application</li><li>Only slogans</li><li>Only unsupported names</li><li>Only a guaranteed conclusion</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An essay should explain and evaluate competing reasoning.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Legal validity and Moral merit.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write a 900-word comparative jurisprudence essay on the fictional unfair rule, presenting and criticising both approaches.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M20:Negotiation, Mediation and Arbitration": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">TIH • Liberian Law: Law School Preparation • Module 20</div><h3>LESSON NOTE: NEGOTIATION, MEDIATION AND ARBITRATION</h3><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Subject</th><th scope=\"col\">Topic</th><th scope=\"col\">Level</th><th scope=\"col\">Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jurisprudence and Dispute Resolution</td><td>Negotiation, Mediation and Arbitration</td><td>Foundation / law-school preparation</td><td>60–90 minutes including reading and exercises</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain negotiation, mediation and arbitration using the distinctions below.</li><li>Apply the lesson concepts to the fictional scenario and test a competing explanation.</li><li>Locate the assigned legal material and state the limits of the available authority.</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Negotiation is direct effort to reach agreement. Mediation uses a neutral facilitator to support voluntary agreement. Arbitration involves a tribunal determination under the applicable agreement and law. They are alternatives with different roles, limits and legal effects.</p><h4>2. Meaning and important distinctions</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Concept</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mediation</td><td>Facilitated effort toward voluntary agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Arbitration</td><td>Adjudication through an authorised private tribunal process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Teaching explanation</h4><p>Identify authority to settle, interests, evidence, alternatives and constraints. A mediator does not automatically impose a binding award. An arbitration clause needs analysis of scope, validity and applicable procedure. Confidentiality cannot be assumed without checking the rules or agreement. Settlement must not conceal coercion or unlawfully override protections. Family violence and serious power imbalances may require a different approach and professional safeguards.</p><h4>4. Worked Liberian classroom example</h4><p>Fictional case: a customer and supplier disagree about defective chairs. Prepare interests, evidence, possible remedies and alternatives before discussing settlement. A mediator helps them explore terms; an arbitrator may decide if lawfully empowered. Write any proposed agreement clearly, including performance and unresolved points. Do not assume signing any document removes all rights or guarantees enforcement.</p><h4>5. Primary reading and legal research</h4><p>Read Chapter 64 on arbitration and identify any applicable agreement and current procedural rules; distinguish mediation as a separate process.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.lla.gov.lr/index.php/downloadable-resources/policies-laws-regulations-repository?download=41:civil-procedure-law\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Civil Procedure Law, Title 1 — Liberia Land Authority repository</a></p><aside class=\"law-callout\"><strong>Jurisdiction check:</strong> The accompanying video may use a foreign legal system. Use it for concepts; test any local proposition against the assigned Liberian sources. Check amendments and current court rules before relying on a deadline, rate, penalty or procedure. Source review: 13 October 2026.</aside><h4>6. Answering an examination question</h4><p>Frame the issue narrowly. Identify a verified applicable rule, explain how each material fact supports or challenges it, and give a conditional conclusion where necessary. In this scenario, address both mediation and arbitration. A definition without application does not answer the problem.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Negotiation is direct effort to reach agreement. Mediation uses a neutral facilitator to support voluntary agreement. Arbitration involves a tribunal determination under the applicable agreement and law. They are alternatives with different roles, limits and legal effects.</li><li>Separate mediation from arbitration.</li><li>Use the actual legal text and make uncertainty visible.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Term</th><th scope=\"col\">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mediation</td><td>Facilitated effort toward voluntary agreement</td></tr><tr><td>Arbitration</td><td>Adjudication through an authorised private tribunal process</td></tr></tbody></table></div><section class=\"law-glossary\" aria-label=\"Key Words and Meanings\"><h4>Key Words &amp; Meanings</h4><p>Tap a term to open its meaning.</p><details><summary>Mediation</summary><p>Facilitated effort toward voluntary agreement</p></details><details><summary>Arbitration</summary><p>Adjudication through an authorised private tribunal process</p></details></section><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><p><strong>Who ordinarily facilitates rather than imposes the parties agreement?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A mediator</li><li>An authorised arbitrator deciding an award</li><li>A judge giving judgment</li><li>A party acting alone</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Mediation centres on voluntary agreement.</p></details><p><strong>What should be checked before relying on an arbitration clause?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its scope, validity and applicable procedure</li><li>Only its font</li><li>Only the room size</li><li>Only the contract length</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An arbitration process requires legal authority and relevant coverage.</p></details><p><strong>Why prepare alternatives before negotiation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They help evaluate proposed settlement terms</li><li>They guarantee a settlement</li><li>They replace all evidence</li><li>They require disclosure of secrets</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal answer and explanation</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A party needs to compare an offer with realistic options.</p></details><h5>Short answer</h5><ul><li>Explain the distinction between Mediation and Arbitration.</li><li>What missing fact in the worked example could change your conclusion, and why?</li></ul><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, argue both sides of the worked example. Identify one claim that requires more evidence.</li><li>Open the assigned reading, record a pinpoint reference, and explain how it supports or limits the example analysis.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Conduct a fictional supplier negotiation and write a settlement term sheet plus a comparison of mediation and arbitration.</p><p><strong>Self-review:</strong> Check accuracy, authority, application, counterargument and clarity. Keep your work in your portfolio; project completion is self-recorded unless a TIH tutor separately reviews it.</p><p class=\"law-footnote\">Independent TIH education. Not legal advice, a law degree, bar admission or an accredited law-school qualification. Use fictional facts and never disclose confidential client information.</p></article>", "M21:Liberian Case Brief and Research Portfolio": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 21 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Liberian Case Brief and Research Portfolio</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>Use one published Supreme Court opinion located through the Judiciary. Produce a case brief and a research log. Do not invent a case citation.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A one-page case brief with the issue, procedural history, holding and reasons</li><li>A five-entry primary-source research log with pinpoint references and status checks</li><li>A 600-word IRAC analysis of a changed fictional fact and one counterargument</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M21:Contracts and Land Due-Diligence Portfolio": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 21 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Contracts and Land Due-Diligence Portfolio</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional cooperative wishes to lease a rural workspace and buy equipment. Land authority and delivery terms are disputed.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A land-category and authority checklist with unresolved title questions</li><li>A lease term sheet and equipment-contract formation timeline</li><li>A 700-word remedies memo distinguishing assumptions from verified rules</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M21:Rights, Employment and Evidence Memorandum": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 21 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Rights, Employment and Evidence Memorandum</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>A fictional worker is dismissed following an accusation based on a cropped message. There is a disputed medical record.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>An employment chronology and Decent Work Act applicability analysis</li><li>An evidence table addressing relevance, authenticity and missing context</li><li>A 900-word balanced memorandum identifying rights, procedures and remedies to research</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>", "M21:Moot Court Capstone and Reasoned Opinion": "<article class=\"law-note\"><div class=\"law-banner\">Module 21 • Practical portfolio</div><h3>Moot Court Capstone and Reasoned Opinion</h3><h4>Scenario</h4><p>Represent each side in a fictional dispute about a supplier contract, corporate authority and defective performance. Then act as the classroom decision-maker.</p><h4>Deliverables</h4><ul><li>A 1,200-word memorial with issues, primary authorities, record references and requested relief</li><li>Two five-minute oral submissions responding to three challenging bench questions each</li><li>A 900-word reasoned opinion and self-assessment against the rubric</li></ul><h4>Assessment rubric</h4><div class=\"law-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Criterion</th><th scope=\"col\">Weight</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence of strong work</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Legal accuracy and authority</td><td>30%</td><td>Correct jurisdiction, verified text and pinpoint references</td></tr><tr><td>Application and counterargument</td><td>30%</td><td>Elements linked to facts with competing arguments considered</td></tr><tr><td>Research and evidence</td><td>20%</td><td>Transparent status checks, reliable records and stated gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Ethics and communication</td><td>20%</td><td>Confidentiality, candid limits and clear organisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Completion checklist</h4><ul><li>Use only fictional or public, appropriately handled material.</li><li>Identify assumptions and verify every cited authority.</li><li>Address both parties strongest positions.</li><li>Self-assess against the rubric and record your reflection in Notes.</li></ul><p>Portfolio work is self-recorded in the existing player. Automated quizzes assess knowledge; no claim of automatic tutor marking is made. Use Print → Save as PDF for an offline copy.</p></article>"};
if(typeof document!=="undefined"&&!document.getElementById("law-note-style")){var s=document.createElement("style");s.id="law-note-style";s.textContent=".law-note{font-size:1rem;line-height:1.8;max-width:100%;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.law-note h3{color:#002868;font-size:1.4rem;line-height:1.4}.law-note h4{color:#002868;margin:1.8rem 0 .6rem}.law-banner{background:linear-gradient(120deg,#002868,#087a9e);color:white;border-radius:12px;padding:1rem;font-weight:700}.law-table-wrap{overflow-x:auto;margin:1rem 0}.law-note table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;min-width:280px}.law-note th,.law-note td{padding:.75rem;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}.law-note th{background:#eff6ff}.law-note details{border:1px solid #bfdbfe;border-radius:10px;padding:.8rem;margin:.7rem 0;background:#f8fbff}.law-note summary{cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#002868;min-height:28px}.law-note a{color:#075985;text-decoration:underline}.law-callout{border-left:4px solid #e31e24;background:#fff7ed;padding:1rem;margin:1.5rem 0}.law-footnote{font-size:.85rem;color:#475569}@media(max-width:480px){.law-note h3{font-size:1.15rem}.law-note td,.law-note th{padding:.5rem}}@media print{.law-note details>*{display:block}.law-note{font-size:11pt}.law-callout{break-inside:avoid}}";document.head.appendChild(s);}
})();
